PathwayData.put("data/reading_writing/units/L05U1.json",{"strand":"reading_writing","level":5,"cefr":"B1a","unit":1,"theme":"Discovering New Places","essential_question":"What do we learn when we explore new places?","schema_version":"1.0","core_words":["explore","explorer","destination","landmark","culture","voyage","guide","souvenir","passport","heritage","region","coastline","valley","discover"],"recycled_words":["souq","lantern","landmark","sandstone","camel","coral","dune","because","so","first","then","simile","summary"],"rubrics":[{"id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","title":"Analytic writing rubric","traits":[{"name":"Ideas","name_ar":"الأفكار","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear, interesting and fully developed with strong details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة وممتعة ومدعومة بتفاصيل قوية."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"My ideas are clear and have enough details.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري واضحة ومعها تفاصيل كافية."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"My main idea is there but needs more details.","descriptor_ar":"فكرتي الرئيسية موجودة لكنها تحتاج إلى تفاصيل أكثر."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"My ideas are unclear or very few.","descriptor_ar":"أفكاري غير واضحة أو قليلة جدًا."}]},{"name":"Organization","name_ar":"التنظيم","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Clear beginning, middle and end; ideas flow with linking words.","descriptor_ar":"بداية ووسط ونهاية واضحة، والأفكار مترابطة بكلمات الربط."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Clear order with some linking words.","descriptor_ar":"ترتيب واضح مع بعض كلمات الربط."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Some order, but parts are mixed up.","descriptor_ar":"يوجد بعض الترتيب لكن بعض الأجزاء غير مرتبة."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Ideas are not in order.","descriptor_ar":"الأفكار غير مرتبة."}]},{"name":"Vocabulary","name_ar":"المفردات","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"I use exact, varied words, including new unit words, correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات دقيقة ومتنوعة، منها كلمات الوحدة الجديدة، بشكل صحيح."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"I use some new words correctly.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم بعض الكلمات الجديدة بشكل صحيح."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"I use simple words; some are repeated or wrong.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات بسيطة، وبعضها مكرر أو غير صحيح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"I use very few words; the meaning is hard to understand.","descriptor_ar":"أستخدم كلمات قليلة جدًا ويصعب فهم المعنى."}]},{"name":"Sentence fluency","name_ar":"سلاسة الجمل","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"My sentences are complete, varied in length and easy to read aloud.","descriptor_ar":"جملي كاملة ومتنوعة الطول وسهلة القراءة بصوت عالٍ."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"Most sentences are complete, with some variety.","descriptor_ar":"معظم جملي كاملة مع بعض التنويع."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Many sentences are short or start the same way.","descriptor_ar":"كثير من جملي قصيرة أو تبدأ بالطريقة نفسها."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Sentences are incomplete or run together.","descriptor_ar":"جملي ناقصة أو متداخلة."}]},{"name":"Conventions","name_ar":"قواعد الكتابة","bands":[{"score":4,"descriptor":"Capitals, punctuation, spelling and grammar are almost always correct.","descriptor_ar":"الحروف الكبيرة وعلامات الترقيم والإملاء والقواعد صحيحة دائمًا تقريبًا."},{"score":3,"descriptor":"A few mistakes that do not affect meaning.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء قليلة لا تؤثر في المعنى."},{"score":2,"descriptor":"Several mistakes; the meaning is sometimes unclear.","descriptor_ar":"عدة أخطاء، والمعنى أحيانًا غير واضح."},{"score":1,"descriptor":"Many mistakes make it hard to read.","descriptor_ar":"أخطاء كثيرة تجعل القراءة صعبة."}]}]}],"lessons":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS1","title":"Word Power: Discovering New Places","lesson_type":"vocabulary","objectives":["read, say and use 14 words about exploring new places and 4 Tier 2 words (navigate, significant, preserve, remarkable)","use mixed context clues – definition, synonym, antonym, example and general sense – to work out new words","explain which kind of clue helped me"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة ونطق واستخدام 14 كلمة عن استكشاف الأماكن الجديدة و4 كلمات أكاديمية.","استخدام أدلة السياق المتنوعة – التعريف والمرادف والضد والمثال والمعنى العام – لفهم الكلمات الجديدة.","شرح نوع الدليل الذي ساعدني."],"success_criteria":["I can use the travel and exploring words correctly in sentences.","I can find a context clue and name its type.","I can check my guess by putting it back into the sentence."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع استخدام كلمات السفر والاستكشاف في جمل بشكل صحيح.","أستطيع إيجاد دليل السياق وتسمية نوعه.","أستطيع التحقق من تخميني بوضعه في الجملة."],"map_area":"Vocabulary: Acquisition and Use","vocabulary":[{"word":"explore","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪkˈsplɔr/","definition":"to travel around a place to learn about it","arabic":"يستكشف","example":"We explored the old streets of Jeddah.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"explorer","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɪkˈsplɔrər/","definition":"a person who travels to new places to discover things","arabic":"مستكشف","example":"Ibn Battuta was a famous explorer.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"destination","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌdɛstəˈneɪʃən/","definition":"the place you are traveling to","arabic":"وجهة","example":"Our destination was AlUla.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"landmark","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈlændˌmɑrk/","definition":"a famous building or place that is easy to see and recognize","arabic":"معلم بارز","example":"The tall clock tower is a landmark in Makkah.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"culture","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkʌltʃər/","definition":"the way of life, art, food and traditions of a group of people","arabic":"ثقافة","example":"Food is an important part of a country's culture.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"voyage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvɔɪɪdʒ/","definition":"a long journey, especially by sea","arabic":"رحلة بحرية طويلة","example":"The voyage across the Red Sea took two days.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"guide","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ɡaɪd/","definition":"a person who shows visitors around a place","arabic":"مرشد","example":"Our guide told us the history of Hegra.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V07","tier":"core"},{"word":"souvenir","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌsuvəˈnɪr/","definition":"something you keep to remember a place you visited","arabic":"تذكار","example":"I bought a small lantern as a souvenir.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V08","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V08","tier":"core"},{"word":"passport","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈpæsˌpɔrt/","definition":"an official document you need to travel to another country","arabic":"جواز سفر","example":"Don't forget your passport at the airport.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V09","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V09","tier":"core"},{"word":"heritage","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈhɛrɪtɪdʒ/","definition":"the history, buildings and traditions passed down from the past","arabic":"التراث","example":"Old Jeddah is part of our national heritage.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V10","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V10","tier":"core"},{"word":"region","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈridʒən/","definition":"a large area of a country or the world","arabic":"منطقة","example":"The Asir region has green mountains.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V11","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V11","tier":"core"},{"word":"coastline","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkoʊstˌlaɪn/","definition":"the land along the edge of the sea","arabic":"الساحل / خط الساحل","example":"Saudi Arabia has a long Red Sea coastline.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V12","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V12","tier":"core"},{"word":"valley","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈvæli/","definition":"low land between hills or mountains","arabic":"وادٍ","example":"Palm trees grow in the valley.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V13","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V13","tier":"core"},{"word":"discover","pos":"verb","ipa":"/dɪˈskʌvər/","definition":"to find or learn something for the first time","arabic":"يكتشف","example":"We discovered a hidden café in the old town.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V14","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V14","tier":"core"},{"word":"navigate","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈnævɪˌɡeɪt/","definition":"to find your way from one place to another","arabic":"يجد طريقه / يتنقل","example":"Sailors navigated by the stars.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V15","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V15","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"significant","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/sɪɡˈnɪfɪkənt/","definition":"important or large enough to notice","arabic":"مهم / ملحوظ","example":"Hegra is a significant historical site.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V16","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V16","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"preserve","pos":"verb","ipa":"/prɪˈzɝv/","definition":"to keep something safe from damage so it lasts","arabic":"يحافظ على","example":"We must preserve old buildings.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V17","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V17","tier":"tier2"},{"word":"remarkable","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/rɪˈmɑrkəbəl/","definition":"unusual and surprising in a way that people notice","arabic":"رائع / لافت","example":"Elephant Rock is a remarkable rock.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V18","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V18","tier":"tier2"}],"explanation":{"text":"When you meet a new word, the sentence or the sentences around it often give you a **clue**. Good readers know **five kinds of context clues**:\n\n(1) **Definition** – the meaning is given: *is, means, is called, or,* commas/dashes. (2) **Synonym** – a word with a similar meaning nearby: *also, like*. (3) **Antonym** – an opposite: *but, unlike, however, not*. (4) **Example** – examples show the meaning: *such as, for example, including*. (5) **General sense** – you use the whole situation and what you know.\n\nSteps: find the new word → look **before and after** it → name the **type of clue** → make a **guess** → put your guess **back in the sentence** to check that it makes sense.","examples":["**Definition:** *Their wooden balconies, called **rawasheen**, hung over the lanes.* → rawasheen = wooden balconies.","**Definition (dash):** *a **souq** – a traditional market – full of spices* → souq = traditional market.","**Synonym:** *The trip was a long **voyage**; the journey across the sea took two days.* → voyage = journey.","**Antonym:** *The new mall was **spacious**, but the old lanes were narrow.* → spacious = wide, with a lot of space.","**Example:** ***Landmarks** such as the Kingdom Tower and the old Nasseef House help visitors find their way.*","**General sense:** *After walking all day in the heat, the explorers were **parched** and drank three bottles of water.* → parched = very thirsty.","**Two clues together:** *Hegra is a **significant** site – one of the most important places in Saudi history.* (definition after the dash)","**Check it:** *The explorers were very thirsty and drank three bottles of water.* ✔ It makes sense.","Clue words to look for: **is, means, or, called** · **also, like, similar to** · **but, unlike, however** · **such as, for example, including**."],"common_errors":["✗ Stopping at the new word and guessing from its sound. → ✓ Read before AND after the word. (Clues can come later.)","✗ Mixing up synonym and antonym clues. → ✓ Look at the signal word: also = similar; but = opposite. (Signal words decide.)","✗ Choosing a meaning that doesn't fit the sentence. → ✓ Put your guess back in the sentence to check. (Always check.)","✗ We visited many landmark. → ✓ We visited many landmarks. (Plural **-s**.)","✗ I bought a souvenir for remember the trip. → ✓ I bought a souvenir to remember the trip. (**to** + verb for purpose.)","✗ The explorer discovered to a cave. → ✓ The explorer discovered a cave. (discover + object (no **to**).)","✗ Old buildings must preserve. → ✓ Old buildings must be preserved. (Passive: **be preserved**.)"],"arabic":"عند مواجهة كلمة جديدة تعطيك الجملة أو الجمل المحيطة بها دليلًا. وهناك خمسة أنواع من أدلة السياق:\n\nالتعريف (is, means, or, called)، والمرادف (also, like)، والضد (but, unlike, however)، والمثال (such as, for example)، والمعنى العام للموقف.\n\nالخطوات: جِد الكلمة ← انظر قبلها وبعدها ← سمِّ نوع الدليل ← خمّن ← أعد التخمين إلى الجملة لتتحقق.\n\nانتبه: discover بلا to، وpreserve في المبني للمجهول: must be preserved."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Pack your bag, grab your passport – we're going to explore new places!","on_screen":"Discovering New Places 🧭","visual":"A backpack, a passport and a compass on a map of Saudi Arabia."},{"time":"00:25–01:15","narration":"Explorers discover new destinations: landmarks, valleys, coastlines and whole regions!","on_screen":"explore · explorer · destination · landmark · valley · coastline · region · discover","visual":"Quick shots: a coastline, a green valley, a tall landmark, a map pin."},{"time":"01:15–01:50","narration":"Every place has its own culture and heritage. A guide helps, and a souvenir helps you remember!","on_screen":"culture · heritage · guide · souvenir · voyage · passport","visual":"A guide in an old town; a child holding a small souvenir."},{"time":"01:50–03:00","narration":"New word? Be a detective! Definition, synonym, antonym, example or general sense – which clue is it?","on_screen":"5 kinds of context clues 🔍","visual":"A magnifying glass moves over five sentences; each clue type lights up in a different color."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Navigate, preserve, significant, remarkable – four big words for big adventures!","on_screen":"Tier 2 words ⭐","visual":"A compass needle spins and points to each word."}],"captions_ar":["جهّز حقيبتك وخذ جواز سفرك؛ سنستكشف أماكن جديدة!","المستكشفون يكتشفون وجهات جديدة: معالم وأودية وسواحل ومناطق كاملة!","لكل مكان ثقافته وتراثه؛ المرشد يساعدك والتذكار يذكّرك!","كلمة جديدة؟ كن محققًا! تعريف أم مرادف أم ضد أم مثال أم معنى عام؟","يتنقل، يحافظ، مهم، رائع: أربع كلمات كبيرة لمغامرات كبيرة!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, adventurous female travel presenter, speed 0.95."},"texts":[],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS1-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Choose the correct word.","instructions_ar":"اختر الكلمة الصحيحة.","items":[{"q":"The place you are traveling to is your ___.","answer":"destination","explanation":"Destination.","explanation_ar":"وجهة.","options":["souvenir","passport","destination","heritage"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Low land between two mountains is a ___.","answer":"valley","explanation":"Valley.","explanation_ar":"وادٍ.","options":["coastline","landmark","voyage","valley"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"A person who shows visitors around a historic site is a ___.","answer":"guide","explanation":"Guide.","explanation_ar":"مرشد.","options":["passport","guide","region","souvenir"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Layla bought a small painted plate to remember Jeddah. It was a ___.","answer":"souvenir","explanation":"Souvenir.","explanation_ar":"تذكار.","options":["souvenir","landmark","voyage","valley"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"You need a ___ to travel from Saudi Arabia to another country.","answer":"passport","explanation":"Passport.","explanation_ar":"جواز سفر.","options":["coastline","heritage","guide","passport"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Saudi Arabia has a long Red Sea ___, with beaches and coral reefs.","answer":"coastline","explanation":"Coastline.","explanation_ar":"ساحل.","options":["valley","coastline","passport","destination"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS1-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Context clues. Choose the meaning and the clue type.","instructions_ar":"أدلة السياق: اختر المعنى ونوع الدليل.","items":[{"q":"*The old houses have wooden balconies, called **rawasheen**.* What are *rawasheen*?","answer":"wooden balconies","explanation":"Definition clue: called.","explanation_ar":"شرفات خشبية.","options":["wooden balconies","stone walls","narrow lanes","old markets"],"dok":1,"skill":"definition clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The new mall was **spacious**, but the old lanes were narrow.* What type of clue helps you?","answer":"antonym clue","explanation":"but = opposite.","explanation_ar":"دليل الضد.","options":["example clue","antonym clue","definition clue","synonym clue"],"dok":2,"skill":"clue types","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*AlUla has many **landmarks**, such as Elephant Rock and the Old Town.* What does *landmarks* mean?","answer":"famous places that are easy to recognize","explanation":"Example clue: such as.","explanation_ar":"معالم بارزة.","options":["famous places that are easy to recognize","shopping bags","kinds of sand","small animals"],"dok":1,"skill":"example clues","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*After hiking all day in the heat, the explorers were **parched** and drank three bottles of water.* *Parched* means ___.","answer":"very thirsty","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"عطشان جدًا.","options":["very cold","very rich","very lost","very thirsty"],"dok":2,"skill":"general sense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*The sailors' **voyage** was long; the journey across the sea took two weeks.* What type of clue is *journey*?","answer":"synonym clue","explanation":"Similar meaning.","explanation_ar":"دليل المرادف.","options":["antonym clue","example clue","synonym clue","no clue"],"dok":2,"skill":"clue types","stage":"guided"},{"q":"*Hegra is a **significant** site – one of the most important places in Saudi history.* What does *significant* mean, and what is the clue?","answer":"important; a definition after the dash","explanation":"Dash definition.","explanation_ar":"مهم؛ تعريف بعد الشرطة.","options":["small; an antonym after but","colorful; an example after such as","important; a definition after the dash","old; a synonym after also"],"dok":3,"skill":"clue types","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS1-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Use the word bank.","instructions_ar":"استخدم بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"Long ago, sailors used the stars to ________ across the sea.","answer":"navigate","explanation":"Find the way.","explanation_ar":"يجدوا طريقهم.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"The government works hard to ________ old buildings in Al-Balad.","answer":"preserve","explanation":"Keep safe.","explanation_ar":"تحافظ على.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Elephant Rock is a ________ rock that really looks like an elephant.","answer":"remarkable","explanation":"Surprising.","explanation_ar":"لافتة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Visiting Hegra was a ________ moment in my life – I will never forget it.","answer":"significant","explanation":"Important.","explanation_ar":"مهمة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Asir is a green, mountainous ________ in the south of Saudi Arabia.","answer":"region","explanation":"Large area.","explanation_ar":"منطقة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Ibn Battuta was a famous Muslim ________ who traveled for almost 30 years.","answer":"explorer","explanation":"Traveler who discovers.","explanation_ar":"مستكشف.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS1-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write with the new words and clues.","instructions_ar":"اكتب بالكلمات الجديدة والأدلة.","items":[{"q":"Write a sentence that gives a DEFINITION clue for *souvenir*.","answer":"I bought a souvenir, something to help me remember the trip, at the old souq.","explanation":"Definition clue.","explanation_ar":"دليل تعريف.","dok":2,"skill":"writing clues","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a sentence that gives an EXAMPLE clue for *heritage*.","answer":"Our heritage includes things such as old houses, traditional clothes and Arabic coffee.","explanation":"Example clue.","explanation_ar":"دليل مثال.","dok":2,"skill":"writing clues","stage":"production"},{"q":"Describe a destination you would like to explore. Use three unit words.","answer":"I'd like to explore the Asir region, hike in its green valleys and visit the colorful village of Rijal Almaa.","explanation":"Three words.","explanation_ar":"ثلاث كلمات.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Why is it important to preserve heritage sites?","answer":"Because they show us how people lived in the past, and if we lose them, we can't build them again.","explanation":"Reason.","explanation_ar":"سبب.","dok":3,"skill":"reasoning","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Clue Compass","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Compass quest: a sentence with a new word appears on an old map; the child chooses the meaning and then the clue type on the compass. Each correct answer moves the explorer one step toward a hidden treasure.","A team spins the compass to choose a clue type.","The team draws sentence cards until it finds one with that clue type.","The team says the meaning of the new word and points to the clue words.","Other teams check by putting the meaning back in the sentence.","Correct meaning = 1 point; correct clue type = 1 point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Which word means *to find your way from one place to another*?","answer":"navigate","explanation":"Navigate.","explanation_ar":"يجد طريقه.","options":["preserve","discover","explore","navigate"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which signal words often show an EXAMPLE clue?","answer":"such as, for example","explanation":"Example clue.","explanation_ar":"such as, for example.","options":["but, unlike","is called, means","also, like","such as, for example"],"dok":1,"skill":"clue types"},{"q":"*Al-Balad is part of our **heritage**.* *Heritage* means ___.","answer":"history and traditions passed down from the past","explanation":"Heritage.","explanation_ar":"التراث.","options":["history and traditions passed down from the past","a new shopping mall","a kind of passport","the weather"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Unlike the busy souq, the museum was **tranquil**.* *Tranquil* means ___.","answer":"calm and quiet","explanation":"Antonym clue.","explanation_ar":"هادئ.","options":["crowded","expensive","calm and quiet","dark"],"dok":2,"skill":"antonym clues"},{"q":"Which sentence about the explorers is correct?","answer":"The explorers discovered a hidden cave.","explanation":"discover + object.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The explorers discovered a hidden cave.","The explorers discovered to a hidden cave.","The explorers discover a hidden cave yesterday.","The explorers discovering a hidden cave."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *preserve* correctly?","answer":"Old buildings must be preserved.","explanation":"Passive.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Old buildings must preserve.","Old buildings must be preserved.","Old buildings must preserving.","Old buildings must to preserve."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"*The guide spoke about the **mural** – a large painting on a wall – in the old town.* What kind of clue is this?","answer":"definition clue","explanation":"Dash definition.","explanation_ar":"دليل التعريف.","options":["definition clue","antonym clue","example clue","general-sense clue"],"dok":2,"skill":"clue types"},{"q":"What should you ALWAYS do after you guess a word's meaning?","answer":"Put the meaning back in the sentence to check it makes sense.","explanation":"Check.","explanation_ar":"تحقق بإعادته للجملة.","options":["Move on without checking.","Count the letters.","Put the meaning back in the sentence to check it makes sense.","Ask what it sounds like."],"dok":2,"skill":"strategy"},{"q":"*The desert was **desolate**: no people, no trees and no sound for kilometers.* What does *desolate* mean?","answer":"empty and lonely","explanation":"General sense.","explanation_ar":"مقفر وموحش.","options":["busy and noisy","empty and lonely","green and wet","cold and snowy"],"dok":3,"skill":"general sense"},{"q":"*Some souvenirs, **like** dates and perfume, are easy to carry home.* What does *like* mean in this sentence?","answer":"for example","explanation":"Multiple meanings + example clue.","explanation_ar":"مثل (على سبيل المثال).","options":["enjoy","the same as","want","for example"],"dok":3,"skill":"example clues"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'explore': a family walking through old lanes with a map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'explorer': an explorer with a compass and a backpack"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'destination': a map with a red pin"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'landmark': a tall famous building above a city"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'culture': traditional food, clothes and music"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'voyage': an old ship on the sea"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'guide': a tour guide talking to visitors"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V08","prompt":"Picture for the word 'souvenir': a small gift from a trip on a shelf"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V09","prompt":"Picture for the word 'passport': a passport and a boarding pass"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V10","prompt":"Picture for the word 'heritage': an old coral-stone house"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V11","prompt":"Picture for the word 'region': a map with one area colored"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V12","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coastline': a long beach seen from the air"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V13","prompt":"Picture for the word 'valley': a green valley between mountains"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V14","prompt":"Picture for the word 'discover': a child finding a hidden door"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V15","prompt":"Picture for the word 'navigate': a compass and a map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V16","prompt":"Picture for the word 'significant': a star sticker on a map"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V17","prompt":"Picture for the word 'preserve': workers carefully repairing an old wall"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V18","prompt":"Picture for the word 'remarkable': a rock shaped like an elephant"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-01","prompt":"A map collage of Saudi destinations with the class characters."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-02","prompt":"Travel objects and a guide."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS1-03","prompt":"A compass showing five kinds of context clues."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V01","script":"explore. We explored the old streets of Jeddah."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V02","script":"explorer. Ibn Battuta was a famous explorer."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V03","script":"destination. Our destination was AlUla."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V04","script":"landmark. The tall clock tower is a landmark in Makkah."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V05","script":"culture. Food is an important part of a country's culture."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V06","script":"voyage. The voyage across the Red Sea took two days."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V07","script":"guide. Our guide told us the history of Hegra."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V08","script":"souvenir. I bought a small lantern as a souvenir."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V09","script":"passport. Don't forget your passport at the airport."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V10","script":"heritage. Old Jeddah is part of our national heritage."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V11","script":"region. The Asir region has green mountains."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V12","script":"coastline. Saudi Arabia has a long Red Sea coastline."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V13","script":"valley. Palm trees grow in the valley."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V14","script":"discover. We discovered a hidden café in the old town."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V15","script":"navigate. Sailors navigated by the stars."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V16","script":"significant. Hegra is a significant historical site."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V17","script":"preserve. We must preserve old buildings."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-V18","script":"remarkable. Elephant Rock is a remarkable rock."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS1-VID","script":"Pack your bag, grab your passport – we're going to explore new places! Explorers discover new destinations: landmarks, valleys, coastlines and whole regions! Every place has its own culture and heritage. A guide helps, and a souvenir helps you remember! New word? Be a detective! Definition, synonym, antonym, example or general sense – which clue is it? Navigate, preserve, significant, remarkable – four big words for big adventures!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2","title":"Lost in Old Jeddah","lesson_type":"close_reading","objectives":["read a realistic story of about 400 words set in historic Jeddah","summarize a story with Somebody–Wanted–But–So–Then (SWBST)","answer literal, inferential and evaluative questions with evidence"],"objectives_ar":["قراءة قصة واقعية من نحو 430 كلمة تدور في جدة التاريخية.","تلخيص القصة باستخدام نموذج: شخص–أراد–لكن–لذلك–ثم.","الإجابة عن أسئلة حرفية واستنتاجية وتقييمية بالدليل."],"success_criteria":["I can identify the main character, her goal and her problem.","I can write a SWBST summary in my own words in 3–4 sentences.","I can support my answers with quotations from the story."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تحديد الشخصية الرئيسية وهدفها ومشكلتها.","أستطيع كتابة ملخص SWBST بكلماتي في 3–4 جمل.","أستطيع دعم إجاباتي باقتباسات من القصة."],"map_area":"Literary Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"coral stone","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɔrəl stoʊn/","definition":"stone made from old sea coral, used to build old Jeddah houses","arabic":"الحجر المنقبي","example":"The old houses are made of coral stone.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"balcony","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈbælkəni/","definition":"a small area outside an upper window, with a wall or a rail around it","arabic":"شرفة","example":"The wooden balcony hung over the lane.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"maze","pos":"noun","ipa":"/meɪz/","definition":"a confusing set of paths where it is easy to get lost","arabic":"متاهة","example":"The alleys twist like a maze.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"alley","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈæli/","definition":"a narrow street between buildings","arabic":"زقاق","example":"We walked down a narrow alley.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"trot","pos":"verb","ipa":"/trɑt/","definition":"to move quickly with short steps","arabic":"يهرول","example":"The cat trotted down the lane.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"echo","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈɛkoʊ/","definition":"(of a sound) to be heard again as it bounces off walls","arabic":"يتردد صداه","example":"The call to prayer echoed between the buildings.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"relief","pos":"noun","ipa":"/rɪˈlif/","definition":"the happy feeling when a worry ends","arabic":"ارتياح","example":"Father laughed with relief.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **story summary** tells the **most important events** in a few sentences, in your own words. It leaves out small details and dialogue.\n\nUse **SWBST**: **Somebody** (the main character) – **Wanted** (her goal) – **But** (the problem) – **So** (what she did to solve it) – **Then** (how it ended). Together, these make a 3–4 sentence summary.\n\nCheck your summary: Does it name the **main character**? Does it include the **problem** and the **solution**? Is it **short** and in **your own words**? Did you leave out **small details** (the cat's white paws, the colors of the balconies)?","examples":["**Somebody:** Layla, a girl who loves to draw.","**Wanted:** to sketch the beautiful rawasheen in Old Jeddah.","**But:** she followed a cat into the twisting alleys and got lost.","**So:** she stayed calm, used the sounds and smells of the spice souq to find her way, and asked a seller for help.","**Then:** she found her family at Nasseef House and learned to notice landmarks.","**Full summary:** *While visiting Old Jeddah, Layla wanted to sketch the beautiful wooden balconies. But she followed a cat into the narrow alleys and got lost. So she stayed calm, followed the smell of the spice souq and asked a seller for directions. Then she found her family at Nasseef House and learned to always notice landmarks.*","Leave out: *The cat had white paws.* / *Father said, \"Next time, follow your family!\"*","Own words: *her heart began to pound* → **she was scared**.","Not a summary: *I think Layla was silly to follow the cat.* (opinion)"],"common_errors":["✗ Retelling every event in order. → ✓ Choose only the SWBST events. (Short!)","✗ Copying dialogue into the summary. → ✓ Report what happened in your own words. (No quotes in a summary.)","✗ Forgetting the ending. → ✓ Add 'Then …' with how it ended. (Beginning to end.)","✗ Adding your opinion. → ✓ Keep opinions for a response, not a summary. (Facts of the story only.)","✗ Layla follow the cat. → ✓ Layla followed the cat. (Past tense in summaries of past events.)","✗ She was lost, so she was lost. → ✓ She was lost, so she followed the smell of the souq. (**So** = what she did.)"],"arabic":"ملخص القصة يذكر أهم الأحداث في جمل قليلة وبكلماتك، دون التفاصيل الصغيرة أو الحوار.\n\nاستخدم نموذج SWBST: شخص (الشخصية الرئيسية) – أراد (هدفها) – لكن (المشكلة) – لذلك (ما فعلته للحل) – ثم (النهاية).\n\nتحقق: هل ذكرت الشخصية الرئيسية والمشكلة والحل؟ هل الملخص قصير وبكلماتك؟ هل حذفت التفاصيل الصغيرة؟\n\nجدة التاريخية (البلد) مسجلة في قائمة التراث العالمي لليونسكو، وتشتهر ببيوت الحجر المنقبي والرواشين الخشبية."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Coral-stone houses, wooden balconies, twisting alleys… Welcome to Old Jeddah! But someone is about to get lost.","on_screen":"Lost in Old Jeddah","visual":"Sunset over the rawasheen of Al-Balad."},{"time":"00:25–02:10","narration":"The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad…","on_screen":"(story)","visual":"Animated story: Layla sketching, the gray cat, the maze of alleys, the call to prayer, the spice souq lights, the spice seller, Nasseef House and its tree."},{"time":"02:10–02:55","narration":"Summarize it with SWBST: Somebody – Wanted – But – So – Then!","on_screen":"S · W · B · S · T","visual":"Five puzzle pieces fill with the summary parts."},{"time":"02:55–03:15","narration":"Leave out the small details: the cat's white paws, the colors of the balconies.","on_screen":"Keep · Cut ✂️","visual":"Small details fly off a page."},{"time":"03:15–03:30","narration":"Think like an explorer: notice the landmarks around you!","on_screen":"Explorer tip 🧭","visual":"Layla points at Nasseef House's tree."}],"captions_ar":["بيوت من الحجر المنقبي، ورواشين خشبية، وأزقة متعرجة… مرحبًا بكم في جدة التاريخية! لكن أحدًا سيضيع.","كانت شمس العصر تميل إلى البرتقالي حين دخلت أسرة ليلى منطقة البلد…","لخّصها بنموذج: شخص – أراد – لكن – لذلك – ثم!","احذف التفاصيل الصغيرة: أقدام القطة البيضاء وألوان الرواشين.","فكّر كالمستكشف: انتبه للمعالم من حولك!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, warm female storyteller, speed 0.95. A kind older male voice for the spice seller."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"Lost in Old Jeddah","body":"The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad, the old heart of Jeddah. Layla stared up at the tall houses made of coral stone. Their wooden balconies, called rawasheen, were painted green, blue and brown, and they hung over the narrow lanes like carved lanterns.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said. \"These alleys twist like a maze. If anyone gets lost, meet us at Nasseef House – the big house with the old tree in front.\"\n\nLayla nodded, but she wasn't really listening. She had already opened her sketchbook. One of the rawasheen had a pattern of tiny stars, and she wanted to draw every one of them.\n\nWhen she looked up, a gray cat with white paws was watching her from a doorway. \"You look just like Qamar,\" she whispered. The cat flicked its tail and trotted down a side lane. Without thinking, Layla followed.\n\nThe lane turned left, then right, then left again. The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky. When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around. Her family was gone.\n\nHer heart began to pound. Every lane looked the same – coral walls, wooden windows, closed doors. Somewhere, the call to the Maghrib prayer began to echo between the buildings.\n\nLayla took a deep breath. \"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself. She closed her eyes and listened. To her right, she could hear the clatter of a busy market. She could smell cardamom and cinnamon. That must be the spice souq, she thought. We walked through it on the way here!\n\nShe followed the smell until the lane opened into Souq Al-Alawi, where lights were coming on above the stalls. An old spice seller noticed her worried face.\n\n\"Are you lost, my daughter?\" he asked kindly.\n\n\"I'm looking for Nasseef House,\" Layla said.\n\nHe pointed down the main street. \"Walk straight. When you see the big tree, you're there.\"\n\nA few minutes later, she saw it: the tall house and the old tree. Mother was standing beneath it with her phone in her hand. She rushed to Layla and hugged her tightly.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Layla said. \"I followed a cat.\"\n\nFather laughed with relief. \"Next time, follow your family!\"\n\nThat night, Layla finished her drawing of the starry rawasheen. It was the best souvenir of her trip – and a reminder to always notice the landmarks around her.","word_count":401,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS2-1","genre":"narrative","role":"main","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"coral stone","definition":"stone made from old coral from the sea, used to build old Jeddah houses","arabic":"الحجر المنقبي (المرجاني)"},{"word":"rawasheen","definition":"carved wooden balconies with small windows on old Hijazi houses","arabic":"الرواشين"},{"word":"maze","definition":"a confusing set of paths where it is easy to get lost","arabic":"متاهة"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the story. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ القصة واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"What are the old houses in Al-Balad made of?","answer":"coral stone","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الحجر المنقبي.","options":["coral stone","wood only","glass and steel","mud bricks"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Where did Father say to meet if anyone got lost?","answer":"at Nasseef House, the big house with the old tree","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"بيت نصيف.","options":["at the car","at Nasseef House, the big house with the old tree","at the spice souq","at the mosque"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did Layla open her sketchbook?","answer":"to draw a balcony with a pattern of tiny stars","explanation":"Paragraph 3.","explanation_ar":"لترسم رَوشنًا بنقوش نجوم.","options":["to write a list","to draw a balcony with a pattern of tiny stars","to find a map","to draw the cat"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did Layla follow the cat?","answer":"It looked just like her cat, Qamar.","explanation":"Paragraph 4.","explanation_ar":"كانت تشبه قطتها قمر.","options":["It was carrying her sketchbook.","Father told her to.","It looked just like her cat, Qamar.","It was hurt."],"dok":2,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which sounds and smells helped Layla find her way?","answer":"the clatter of a market and the smell of cardamom and cinnamon","explanation":"Paragraph 7.","explanation_ar":"صوت السوق ورائحة الهيل والقرفة.","options":["the sea and the wind","music and perfume","cars and smoke","the clatter of a market and the smell of cardamom and cinnamon"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who gave Layla directions?","answer":"an old spice seller","explanation":"Paragraphs 8–11.","explanation_ar":"بائع بهارات مسن.","options":["a police officer","her brother","an old spice seller","the cat's owner"],"dok":1,"skill":"characters","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Summarizing with SWBST. Choose.","instructions_ar":"التلخيص بنموذج SWBST: اختر.","items":[{"q":"In SWBST, what did Layla WANT?","answer":"to sketch the beautiful rawasheen","explanation":"Goal.","explanation_ar":"أن ترسم الرواشين.","options":["to buy spices","to sketch the beautiful rawasheen","to find a cat for her home","to visit the sea"],"dok":1,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What is the BUT (the problem) in the story?","answer":"She followed a cat into the alleys and got lost.","explanation":"Problem.","explanation_ar":"تبعت قطة وضاعت.","options":["The souq was closed.","Her sketchbook got wet.","Nasseef House had no tree.","She followed a cat into the alleys and got lost."],"dok":1,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence BEST fits the SO part of SWBST?","answer":"She stayed calm, followed the sounds and smells of the souq and asked for directions.","explanation":"Solution.","explanation_ar":"الحل.","options":["The cat had white paws.","The balconies were green and blue.","She stayed calm, followed the sounds and smells of the souq and asked for directions.","Father laughed with relief."],"dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which detail should you LEAVE OUT of a short summary of Layla's adventure?","answer":"The cat flicked its tail.","explanation":"Small detail.","explanation_ar":"تفصيل صغير.","options":["The cat flicked its tail.","Layla got lost.","She found her family at Nasseef House.","She used the souq to find her way."],"dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST summary of *Lost in Old Jeddah*?","answer":"In Old Jeddah, Layla wanted to sketch the balconies, but she followed a cat and got lost. So she used the sounds and smells of the souq and asked for help. Then she found her family at Nasseef House.","explanation":"SWBST.","explanation_ar":"الملخص الأفضل.","options":["Layla likes cats and drawing and the color green.","In Old Jeddah, Layla wanted to sketch the balconies, but she followed a cat and got lost. So she used the sounds and smells of the souq and asked for help. Then she found her family at Nasseef House.","The houses are made of coral stone. The souq smells of cardamom. The tree is old.","I think Layla was silly, and I would never follow a cat."],"dok":3,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Why should a summary NOT include dialogue?","answer":"A summary reports the main events briefly in your own words.","explanation":"Summary rules.","explanation_ar":"الملخص بكلماتك ومختصر.","options":["Dialogue is always wrong.","Characters don't talk in stories.","It makes the summary too short.","A summary reports the main events briefly in your own words."],"dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2-A3","type":"highlight","stage":"freer","instructions":"Find the evidence: Find the evidence.","instructions_ar":"ابحث عن الدليل.","items":[{"q":"Underline the sentence that shows the exact moment Layla realized she was lost.","answer":"When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around. Her family was gone.","explanation":"Turning point.","explanation_ar":"لحظة الإدراك.","evidence":"When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around. Her family was gone.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS2-1","dok":1,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad, the old heart of Jeddah.","Layla stared up at the tall houses made of coral stone.","Their wooden balconies, called rawasheen, were painted green, blue and brown, and they hung over the narrow lanes like carved lanterns.","\"Stay close,\" Father said.","\"These alleys twist like a maze.","If anyone gets lost, meet us at Nasseef House – the big house with the old tree in front.\"","Layla nodded, but she wasn't really listening.","She had already opened her sketchbook.","One of the rawasheen had a pattern of tiny stars, and she wanted to draw every one of them.","When she looked up, a gray cat with white paws was watching her from a doorway.","\"You look just like Qamar,\" she whispered.","The cat flicked its tail and trotted down a side lane.","Without thinking, Layla followed.","The lane turned left, then right, then left again.","The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky.","When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around.","Her family was gone.","Her heart began to pound.","Every lane looked the same – coral walls, wooden windows, closed doors.","Somewhere, the call to the Maghrib prayer began to echo between the buildings.","Layla took a deep breath.","\"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself.","She closed her eyes and listened.","To her right, she could hear the clatter of a busy market.","She could smell cardamom and cinnamon.","That must be the spice souq, she thought.","We walked through it on the way here!","She followed the smell until the lane opened into Souq Al-Alawi, where lights were coming on above the stalls.","An old spice seller noticed her worried face.","\"Are you lost, my daughter?\" he asked kindly.","\"I'm looking for Nasseef House,\" Layla said.","He pointed down the main street.","\"Walk straight.","When you see the big tree, you're there.\"","A few minutes later, she saw it: the tall house and the old tree.","Mother was standing beneath it with her phone in her hand.","She rushed to Layla and hugged her tightly.","\"I'm sorry,\" Layla said.","\"I followed a cat.\"","Father laughed with relief.","\"Next time, follow your family!\"","That night, Layla finished her drawing of the starry rawasheen.","It was the best souvenir of her trip – and a reminder to always notice the landmarks around her."]},{"q":"Underline the words that show Layla decided to stay calm.","answer":"Layla took a deep breath. \"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself.","explanation":"Calm decision.","explanation_ar":"قرار الهدوء.","evidence":"Layla took a deep breath. \"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself.","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS2-1","dok":2,"skill":"evidence","stage":"freer","sentences":["The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad, the old heart of Jeddah.","Layla stared up at the tall houses made of coral stone.","Their wooden balconies, called rawasheen, were painted green, blue and brown, and they hung over the narrow lanes like carved lanterns.","\"Stay close,\" Father said.","\"These alleys twist like a maze.","If anyone gets lost, meet us at Nasseef House – the big house with the old tree in front.\"","Layla nodded, but she wasn't really listening.","She had already opened her sketchbook.","One of the rawasheen had a pattern of tiny stars, and she wanted to draw every one of them.","When she looked up, a gray cat with white paws was watching her from a doorway.","\"You look just like Qamar,\" she whispered.","The cat flicked its tail and trotted down a side lane.","Without thinking, Layla followed.","The lane turned left, then right, then left again.","The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky.","When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around.","Her family was gone.","Her heart began to pound.","Every lane looked the same – coral walls, wooden windows, closed doors.","Somewhere, the call to the Maghrib prayer began to echo between the buildings.","Layla took a deep breath.","\"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself.","She closed her eyes and listened.","To her right, she could hear the clatter of a busy market.","She could smell cardamom and cinnamon.","That must be the spice souq, she thought.","We walked through it on the way here!","She followed the smell until the lane opened into Souq Al-Alawi, where lights were coming on above the stalls.","An old spice seller noticed her worried face.","\"Are you lost, my daughter?\" he asked kindly.","\"I'm looking for Nasseef House,\" Layla said.","He pointed down the main street.","\"Walk straight.","When you see the big tree, you're there.\"","A few minutes later, she saw it: the tall house and the old tree.","Mother was standing beneath it with her phone in her hand.","She rushed to Layla and hugged her tightly.","\"I'm sorry,\" Layla said.","\"I followed a cat.\"","Father laughed with relief.","\"Next time, follow your family!\"","That night, Layla finished her drawing of the starry rawasheen.","It was the best souvenir of her trip – and a reminder to always notice the landmarks around her."]},{"q":"What does *Her heart began to pound* tell you about Layla?","answer":"She was frightened.","explanation":"Figurative clue.","explanation_ar":"كانت خائفة.","options":["She was running a race.","She was hungry.","She was frightened.","She was bored."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Why does the writer describe the alleys *like a maze*?","answer":"to show how confusing and easy to get lost in they are","explanation":"Simile.","explanation_ar":"ليُظهر أنها مربكة.","options":["to show how confusing and easy to get lost in they are","to show they are full of games","to show they are wide and straight","to show they are new"],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Summarize and respond.","instructions_ar":"لخّص واستجب.","items":[{"q":"Write the SWBST parts in short notes.","answer":"S: Layla. W: to sketch the rawasheen. B: followed a cat and got lost. S: stayed calm, followed the spice smell, asked a seller. T: found her family at Nasseef House.","explanation":"SWBST notes.","explanation_ar":"ملاحظات SWBST.","dok":2,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a 3–4 sentence summary of the story in your own words.","answer":"While visiting Old Jeddah, Layla wanted to draw the wooden balconies. But she followed a cat and got lost in the narrow alleys. So she stayed calm, followed the smell of the spice souq and asked an old seller for directions. Then she found her family at Nasseef House.","explanation":"Summary.","explanation_ar":"ملخص.","dok":3,"skill":"summarizing","stage":"production"},{"q":"How did Layla act like an explorer? Give two pieces of evidence.","answer":"She used her senses: 'she could hear the clatter of a busy market' and 'she could smell cardamom and cinnamon.'","explanation":"Evidence.","explanation_ar":"الدليل.","dok":3,"skill":"evidence","stage":"production"},{"q":"What lesson did Layla learn? Do you agree it is important? Why?","answer":"She learned to notice landmarks. I agree, because landmarks help you find your way in new places.","explanation":"Lesson + opinion.","explanation_ar":"الدرس + رأي.","dok":3,"skill":"evaluate","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS2-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"Lost in Old Jeddah\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “Lost in Old Jeddah”.","answer":"401 words","title":"Lost in Old Jeddah","body":"The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad, the old heart of Jeddah. Layla stared up at the tall houses made of coral stone. Their wooden balconies, called rawasheen, were painted green, blue and brown, and they hung over the narrow lanes like carved lanterns.\n\n\"Stay close,\" Father said. \"These alleys twist like a maze. If anyone gets lost, meet us at Nasseef House – the big house with the old tree in front.\"\n\nLayla nodded, but she wasn't really listening. She had already opened her sketchbook. One of the rawasheen had a pattern of tiny stars, and she wanted to draw every one of them.\n\nWhen she looked up, a gray cat with white paws was watching her from a doorway. \"You look just like Qamar,\" she whispered. The cat flicked its tail and trotted down a side lane. Without thinking, Layla followed.\n\nThe lane turned left, then right, then left again. The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky. When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around. Her family was gone.\n\nHer heart began to pound. Every lane looked the same – coral walls, wooden windows, closed doors. Somewhere, the call to the Maghrib prayer began to echo between the buildings.\n\nLayla took a deep breath. \"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself. She closed her eyes and listened. To her right, she could hear the clatter of a busy market. She could smell cardamom and cinnamon. That must be the spice souq, she thought. We walked through it on the way here!\n\nShe followed the smell until the lane opened into Souq Al-Alawi, where lights were coming on above the stalls. An old spice seller noticed her worried face.\n\n\"Are you lost, my daughter?\" he asked kindly.\n\n\"I'm looking for Nasseef House,\" Layla said.\n\nHe pointed down the main street. \"Walk straight. When you see the big tree, you're there.\"\n\nA few minutes later, she saw it: the tall house and the old tree. Mother was standing beneath it with her phone in her hand. She rushed to Layla and hugged her tightly.\n\n\"I'm sorry,\" Layla said. \"I followed a cat.\"\n\nFather laughed with relief. \"Next time, follow your family!\"\n\nThat night, Layla finished her drawing of the starry rawasheen. It was the best souvenir of her trip – and a reminder to always notice the landmarks around her.","words":401,"limit":255}],"time_limit_sec":255}],"game":{"name":"SWBST Treasure Hunt","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Treasure map: the child taps five 'X' spots on a map of Old Jeddah; each spot hides a sentence. The child drags each sentence into the S, W, B, S, T slots; a correct summary opens the treasure chest.","Each team gets a treasure map with five locations in the classroom.","At each location, they find a sentence card.","They decide which SWBST part it is and put the cards in order.","They read the summary aloud.","Correct order = 5 points; finishing first = 2 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"Where does *Lost in Old Jeddah* take place?","answer":"in Al-Balad, the historic part of Jeddah","explanation":"Setting.","explanation_ar":"في البلد بجدة.","options":["in AlUla","in a desert camp","in a school","in Al-Balad, the historic part of Jeddah"],"dok":1,"skill":"setting"},{"q":"What does SWBST stand for?","answer":"Somebody, Wanted, But, So, Then","explanation":"Strategy.","explanation_ar":"شخص، أراد، لكن، لذلك، ثم.","options":["Start, Write, Begin, Stop, Think","Somebody, Wanted, But, So, Then","See, Watch, Bring, Send, Tell","Story, Words, Book, Setting, Title"],"dok":1,"skill":"summarizing"},{"q":"*The alleys twist like a **maze**.* A *maze* is ___.","answer":"a confusing set of paths","explanation":"Glossary.","explanation_ar":"متاهة.","options":["a big market","a wooden balcony","a confusing set of paths","a tall tree"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"*Father laughed with **relief**.* *Relief* is the feeling when ___.","answer":"a worry ends","explanation":"Relief.","explanation_ar":"زوال القلق.","options":["you are very angry","a worry ends","you are very hungry","you lose something"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"Which sentence for a summary of the story is in the correct tense?","answer":"Layla followed a gray cat into the alleys.","explanation":"Past tense.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Layla followed a gray cat into the alleys.","Layla follow a gray cat into the alleys.","Layla following a gray cat into the alleys.","Layla will followed a gray cat into the alleys."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which part of SWBST is *Then she found her family at Nasseef House*?","answer":"Then","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"ثم.","options":["Wanted","But","Then","Somebody"],"dok":1,"skill":"summarizing"},{"q":"Why was it smart of Father to choose Nasseef House as a meeting place?","answer":"It is a famous landmark that is easy to find.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"لأنه معلم مشهور.","options":["It sells spices.","It is next to the sea.","It is a famous landmark that is easy to find.","It has no tree."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference"},{"q":"What can you infer about the spice seller?","answer":"He is kind and helpful to strangers.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"لطيف ومساعد.","options":["He is kind and helpful to strangers.","He is angry with children.","He is lost too.","He owns the cat."],"dok":2,"skill":"character traits"},{"q":"Which detail BEST shows that the setting made it easy to get lost?","answer":"The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky.","explanation":"Setting evidence.","explanation_ar":"الجدران العالية.","options":["Layla opened her sketchbook.","Mother had her phone in her hand.","The rawasheen were green and blue.","The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky."],"dok":3,"skill":"setting"},{"q":"Which is a better TITLE for a summary of the story's lesson?","answer":"Notice the Landmarks","explanation":"Main idea.","explanation_ar":"انتبه للمعالم.","options":["Notice the Landmarks","The Gray Cat's Paws","Green and Blue Balconies","A Phone in Mother's Hand"],"dok":3,"skill":"theme"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coral stone': a wall of white coral stone blocks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'balcony': a carved wooden balcony"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'maze': a maze seen from above"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'alley': a narrow alley between old houses"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'trot': a cat trotting"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'echo': sound waves bouncing between walls"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'relief': a father smiling and hugging a child"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-01","prompt":"Layla lost in a narrow alley of Old Jeddah."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-02","prompt":"A spice seller giving Layla directions."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS2-03","prompt":"A SWBST summary chart."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V01","script":"coral stone. The old houses are made of coral stone."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V02","script":"balcony. The wooden balcony hung over the lane."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V03","script":"maze. The alleys twist like a maze."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V04","script":"alley. We walked down a narrow alley."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V05","script":"trot. The cat trotted down the lane."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V06","script":"echo. The call to prayer echoed between the buildings."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-V07","script":"relief. Father laughed with relief."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-T1","script":"The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad, the old heart of Jeddah. Layla stared up at the tall houses made of coral stone. Their wooden balconies, called rawasheen, were painted green, blue and brown, and they hung over the narrow lanes like carved lanterns. \"Stay close,\" Father said. \"These alleys twist like a maze. If anyone gets lost, meet us at Nasseef House – the big house with the old tree in front.\" Layla nodded, but she wasn't really listening. She had already opened her sketchbook. One of the rawasheen had a pattern of tiny stars, and she wanted to draw every one of them. When she looked up, a gray cat with white paws was watching her from a doorway. \"You look just like Qamar,\" she whispered. The cat flicked its tail and trotted down a side lane. Without thinking, Layla followed. The lane turned left, then right, then left again. The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky. When the cat slipped through a gate, Layla spun around. Her family was gone. Her heart began to pound. Every lane looked the same – coral walls, wooden windows, closed doors. Somewhere, the call to the Maghrib prayer began to echo between the buildings. Layla took a deep breath. \"Think like an explorer,\" she told herself. She closed her eyes and listened. To her right, she could hear the clatter of a busy market. She could smell cardamom and cinnamon. That must be the spice souq, she thought. We walked through it on the way here! She followed the smell until the lane opened into Souq Al-Alawi, where lights were coming on above the stalls. An old spice seller noticed her worried face. \"Are you lost, my daughter?\" he asked kindly. \"I'm looking for Nasseef House,\" Layla said. He pointed down the main street. \"Walk straight. When you see the big tree, you're there.\" A few minutes later, she saw it: the tall house and the old tree. Mother was standing beneath it with her phone in her hand. She rushed to Layla and hugged her tightly. \"I'm sorry,\" Layla said. \"I followed a cat.\" Father laughed with relief. \"Next time, follow your family!\" That night, Layla finished her drawing of the starry rawasheen. It was the best souvenir of her trip – and a reminder to always notice the landmarks around her."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS2-VID","script":"Coral-stone houses, wooden balconies, twisting alleys… Welcome to Old Jeddah! But someone is about to get lost. The afternoon sun was turning orange when Layla's family entered Al-Balad… Summarize it with SWBST: Somebody – Wanted – But – So – Then! Leave out the small details: the cat's white paws, the colors of the balconies. Think like an explorer: notice the landmarks around you!"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3","title":"Setting and its effect on events: \"AlUla: A Museum Without Walls\"","lesson_type":"strategy_paired_text","objectives":["describe the settings of a story and an informational article","explain how the setting affects events, characters and history (setting → effect)","compare how the two texts use setting"],"objectives_ar":["وصف المكان والزمان في قصة ومقال معلوماتي.","شرح كيف يؤثر المكان والزمان في الأحداث والشخصيات والتاريخ.","مقارنة طريقة استخدام النصين للمكان."],"success_criteria":["I can find details that describe a setting (place, time, weather, features).","I can explain how a feature of the setting causes something to happen.","I can compare the role of setting in a story and in an article."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع إيجاد تفاصيل تصف المكان والزمان.","أستطيع شرح كيف تسبب صفة في المكان حدوث شيء.","أستطيع مقارنة دور المكان في القصة والمقال."],"map_area":"Informational Text: Key Ideas and Details","vocabulary":[{"word":"sandstone","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈsændˌstoʊn/","definition":"a soft rock made of sand pressed together","arabic":"الحجر الرملي","example":"The tombs were carved into sandstone.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"trade route","pos":"noun","ipa":"/treɪd rut/","definition":"a road or path that traders used to carry goods","arabic":"طريق تجاري","example":"AlUla was on an old trade route.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"frankincense","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈfræŋkɪnˌsɛns/","definition":"a sweet-smelling tree sap burned as incense","arabic":"اللبان","example":"Traders carried frankincense to faraway lands.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"tomb","pos":"noun","ipa":"/tum/","definition":"a large stone place where a dead person is buried","arabic":"مقبرة / ضريح","example":"The Nabataeans carved tombs into the rocks.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"carve","pos":"verb","ipa":"/kɑrv/","definition":"to cut a shape into wood or stone","arabic":"ينحت","example":"Workers carved doorways into the cliffs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"ancient","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈeɪnʃənt/","definition":"very, very old; from thousands of years ago","arabic":"قديم جدًا / أثري","example":"Ancient writings cover the cliffs.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"reflect","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈflɛkt/","definition":"to throw back light or an image, like a mirror","arabic":"يعكس","example":"The mirrors reflect the desert.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"The **setting** is **where** and **when** something happens: the place, the time of day or year, the weather and special features (narrow lanes, high walls, sandstone cliffs).\n\nA setting is not just a background. It can **cause** events, **create problems**, **help solve them** and **change how characters feel**. Ask: **What is special about this place or time? What happens because of it?**\n\nInformational texts have settings too. In history and geography, **where** a place is can explain **why** things happened there. Use a **Setting → Effect** sentence: *Because …, …* / *… , so …*.","examples":["Story – **feature:** *The walls were so high that she could see only a thin strip of sky.* → **effect:** Layla couldn't see landmarks, so she got lost.","Story – **feature:** *These alleys twist like a maze.* → **effect:** it was easy to get separated.","Story – **time:** sunset and the Maghrib call to prayer → **effect:** it was getting dark, so Layla felt more worried.","Story – **feature:** the smells and sounds of the spice souq → **effect:** they helped Layla find her way.","Article – **location:** AlUla lay on the Incense Road → **effect:** traders passed through, so kingdoms grew rich.","Article – **feature:** soft sandstone → **effect:** the Nabataeans could carve tombs into it.","Article – **time:** millions of years of wind and water → **effect:** rocks like Elephant Rock formed.","Compare: in the story, the setting **creates a problem**; in the article, the setting **explains history**.","Sentence frame: ***Because AlUla was on the Incense Road, kingdoms grew rich from trade.***"],"common_errors":["✗ Thinking the setting is only the place's name. → ✓ Include time, weather and special features too. (Setting = where + when + features.)","✗ Describing the setting with no effect. → ✓ Add what happens because of it. (Setting → effect.)","✗ Because AlUla on the trade route. → ✓ Because AlUla was on the trade route, kingdoms grew rich. (A full sentence with a verb.)","✗ The tombs carved 2,000 years ago. → ✓ The tombs were carved about 2,000 years ago. (Passive: **were carved**.)","✗ The mirrors reflects the desert. → ✓ The mirrors reflect the desert. (Plural → **reflect**.)","✗ Elephant Rock was carved by people. → ✓ Elephant Rock was shaped by wind and water. (Check the facts!)"],"arabic":"المكان والزمان (البيئة) هو أين ومتى يحدث الشيء: المكان والوقت والطقس والسمات الخاصة.\n\nالبيئة ليست خلفية فقط؛ فقد تسبب الأحداث وتصنع المشكلات وتساعد على حلها وتغيّر مشاعر الشخصيات.\n\nفي النصوص المعلوماتية يفسّر موقعُ المكان سببَ ما حدث فيه، مثل: لأن العلا كانت على طريق البخور ازدهرت ممالكها.\n\nانتبه: صخرة الفيل شكّلتها الرياح والمياه، أما مقابر الحِجر فنحتها الأنباط."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Golden cliffs, a green valley, rocks shaped like animals… Welcome to AlUla, a museum without walls!","on_screen":"AlUla 🏜️🏛️","visual":"Aerial view of AlUla's sandstone mountains and palm valley."},{"time":"00:25–01:50","narration":"In the northwest of Saudi Arabia, golden sandstone mountains rise…","on_screen":"(article, section by section)","visual":"A caravan on the Incense Road; carved tombs at Hegra; writings on the cliffs of Jabal Ikmah; Elephant Rock at sunset; Maraya's mirrors."},{"time":"01:50–02:40","narration":"Setting is more than a place! It can cause events. Because AlUla was on a trade route, kingdoms grew rich.","on_screen":"Setting → Effect","visual":"An arrow from a map of the Incense Road to piles of gold and spices."},{"time":"02:40–03:15","narration":"Remember Layla in Old Jeddah? The high walls and twisting alleys made her get lost!","on_screen":"Story setting → problem","visual":"Split screen: Layla's alley and AlUla's cliffs."},{"time":"03:15–03:30","narration":"Every place has a story. What will you discover?","on_screen":"Preserve and explore 🧭","visual":"Visitors walking carefully among the tombs."}],"captions_ar":["منحدرات ذهبية وواد أخضر وصخور على هيئة حيوانات… مرحبًا بكم في العلا، متحف بلا جدران!","في شمال غرب المملكة ترتفع جبال الحجر الرملي الذهبية…","البيئة أكثر من مكان! قد تسبب الأحداث: لأن العلا كانت على طريق تجاري ازدهرت ممالكها.","أتتذكر ليلى في جدة التاريخية؟ الجدران العالية والأزقة المتعرجة جعلتها تضيع!","لكل مكان قصة. ماذا ستكتشف؟"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, documentary male voice, speed 0.95."},"texts":[{"type":"reading","title":"AlUla: A Museum Without Walls","body":"In the northwest of Saudi Arabia, golden sandstone mountains rise above a green valley of palm trees. This is AlUla, a region so full of history that people call it \"a museum without walls.\"\n\n**A Crossroads of Kingdoms** – Thousands of years ago, AlUla lay on the Incense Road, a trade route that carried frankincense, spices and other goods between southern Arabia and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. Because so many traders passed through, kingdoms such as Dadan and Lihyan grew rich here.\n\n**Hegra** – About 2,000 years ago, the Nabataeans – the same people who built Petra in Jordan – carved more than 100 grand tombs into the soft sandstone at Hegra. In 2008, Hegra became the first UNESCO World Heritage Site in Saudi Arabia.\n\n**An Open Library** – At Jabal Ikmah, hundreds of ancient writings cover the cliffs. Experts call it an \"open library\" because it shows how people wrote long ago.\n\n**Nature's Sculptures** – Over millions of years, wind and water have shaped the rocks into amazing forms. The most famous is Elephant Rock, which looks like a giant elephant with its trunk touching the ground.\n\n**Old and New** – Visitors can walk through the mud-brick Old Town and then attend a concert at Maraya, a huge building covered in mirrors that reflect the desert.\n\nToday, Saudi Arabia is working to preserve AlUla, so that future explorers can discover its secrets too.","word_count":230,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS3-1","genre":"informational","role":"paired","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[{"word":"sandstone","definition":"a soft rock made of sand pressed together","arabic":"الحجر الرملي"},{"word":"frankincense","definition":"a sweet-smelling tree sap burned as incense","arabic":"اللبان"},{"word":"tomb","definition":"a large stone place where a dead person is buried","arabic":"مقبرة / ضريح"}]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read *AlUla: A Museum Without Walls*. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ المقال واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where is AlUla?","answer":"in the northwest of Saudi Arabia","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"شمال غرب المملكة.","options":["on the coast of the Red Sea in Jeddah","in the northwest of Saudi Arabia","in the Empty Quarter","in Jordan"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","text_id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS3-1","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why do people call AlUla *a museum without walls*?","answer":"It is so full of history that the whole region is like a museum.","explanation":"Introduction.","explanation_ar":"لأنها مليئة بالتاريخ.","options":["It is so full of history that the whole region is like a museum.","It has a museum with no roof.","It has no buildings.","Its museum is closed."],"dok":2,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the Incense Road carry?","answer":"frankincense, spices and other goods","explanation":"Crossroads section.","explanation_ar":"اللبان والتوابل وغيرها.","options":["only water","cars and trucks","frankincense, spices and other goods","books only"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Who carved the tombs at Hegra?","answer":"the Nabataeans","explanation":"Hegra section.","explanation_ar":"الأنباط.","options":["the people of Lihyan","the Nabataeans","modern artists","the wind"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What happened in 2008?","answer":"Hegra became the first UNESCO World Heritage Site in Saudi Arabia.","explanation":"Hegra section.","explanation_ar":"سُجلت الحِجر في اليونسكو.","options":["Elephant Rock was carved.","Maraya was destroyed.","The Incense Road opened.","Hegra became the first UNESCO World Heritage Site in Saudi Arabia."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What is Maraya?","answer":"a huge building covered in mirrors","explanation":"Old and New.","explanation_ar":"مبنى مغطى بالمرايا.","options":["an old tomb","a rock shaped like an elephant","a trade route","a huge building covered in mirrors"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Setting and its effect. Choose.","instructions_ar":"المكان وأثره: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Why did kingdoms such as Dadan and Lihyan grow rich?","answer":"Many traders passed through AlUla on the Incense Road.","explanation":"Setting → effect.","explanation_ar":"لمرور التجار.","options":["They found gold in the sea.","Many traders passed through AlUla on the Incense Road.","It rained every day.","They built Maraya."],"dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which feature of Hegra's setting made carving tombs possible?","answer":"the soft sandstone","explanation":"Feature → effect.","explanation_ar":"الحجر الرملي اللين.","options":["the green palm trees","the soft sandstone","the mirrors","the cold winters"],"dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What shaped Elephant Rock?","answer":"wind and water over millions of years","explanation":"Nature's Sculptures.","explanation_ar":"الرياح والمياه.","options":["the Nabataeans","modern machines","traders on camels","wind and water over millions of years"],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"In *Lost in Old Jeddah*, how did the high walls affect Layla?","answer":"She could see only a thin strip of sky and couldn't find landmarks.","explanation":"Story setting.","explanation_ar":"لم تستطع رؤية المعالم.","options":["She could see only a thin strip of sky and couldn't find landmarks.","They kept her warm.","They helped her see the sea.","They made the cat run away."],"dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which part of the Jeddah setting HELPED Layla solve her problem?","answer":"the smells and sounds of the spice souq","explanation":"Setting helps.","explanation_ar":"روائح السوق وأصواته.","options":["the smells and sounds of the spice souq","the high coral walls","the twisting lanes","the closed doors"],"dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"guided"},{"q":"How is the role of setting DIFFERENT in the two texts?","answer":"In the story, the setting creates a problem; in the article, it explains history.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"في القصة يصنع مشكلة وفي المقال يفسّر التاريخ.","options":["The story has no setting.","The article has no place.","In the story, the setting creates a problem; in the article, it explains history.","Both settings are in Jeddah."],"dok":3,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Complete the Setting → Effect Chart.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جدول المكان ← الأثر.","items":[{"q":"Story – narrow, twisting alleys with high walls → effect?","answer":"Layla can't see far or find any landmarks, so she gets lost.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"الأثر.","dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Story – sunset and the Maghrib call to prayer → effect?","answer":"It is getting dark, so Layla feels more worried, and the lights come on in the souq.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"الأثر.","dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Article – AlUla on the Incense Road → effect?","answer":"Many traders pass through, so kingdoms like Dadan and Lihyan grow rich.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"الأثر.","dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Article – soft sandstone cliffs → effect?","answer":"The Nabataeans can carve more than 100 grand tombs at Hegra.","explanation":"Effect.","explanation_ar":"الأثر.","dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Explain and compare.","instructions_ar":"اشرح وقارن.","items":[{"q":"Write a *Because …,* sentence about AlUla's setting.","answer":"Because AlUla was on the Incense Road, its kingdoms grew rich from trade.","explanation":"Because sentence.","explanation_ar":"جملة because.","dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"production"},{"q":"How would *Lost in Old Jeddah* change if it happened in a wide, open park?","answer":"Layla could see her family from far away, so she probably wouldn't get lost at all.","explanation":"Change the setting.","explanation_ar":"غيّر المكان.","dok":3,"skill":"setting and effect","stage":"production"},{"q":"Both texts are about heritage places. How are Al-Balad and AlUla alike?","answer":"Both are very old places in Saudi Arabia with special buildings, and both are protected so people can still visit them.","explanation":"Compare.","explanation_ar":"قارن.","dok":3,"skill":"compare texts","stage":"production"},{"q":"Which place would you rather explore: Al-Balad or AlUla? Why?","answer":"AlUla, because I want to see the carved tombs at Hegra and Elephant Rock.","explanation":"Opinion + reason.","explanation_ar":"رأي + سبب.","dok":2,"skill":"connect","stage":"production"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-TR-1","type":"timed_reading","instructions":"Timed reading: Read \"AlUla: A Museum Without Walls\" aloud or silently. Try to finish in the time.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ النص وحاول أن تنهيه في الوقت المحدد.","items":[{"q":"Read “AlUla: A Museum Without Walls”.","answer":"230 words","title":"AlUla: A Museum Without Walls","body":"In the northwest of Saudi Arabia, golden sandstone mountains rise above a green valley of palm trees. This is AlUla, a region so full of history that people call it \"a museum without walls.\"\n\n**A Crossroads of Kingdoms** – Thousands of years ago, AlUla lay on the Incense Road, a trade route that carried frankincense, spices and other goods between southern Arabia and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. Because so many traders passed through, kingdoms such as Dadan and Lihyan grew rich here.\n\n**Hegra** – About 2,000 years ago, the Nabataeans – the same people who built Petra in Jordan – carved more than 100 grand tombs into the soft sandstone at Hegra. In 2008, Hegra became the first UNESCO World Heritage Site in Saudi Arabia.\n\n**An Open Library** – At Jabal Ikmah, hundreds of ancient writings cover the cliffs. Experts call it an \"open library\" because it shows how people wrote long ago.\n\n**Nature's Sculptures** – Over millions of years, wind and water have shaped the rocks into amazing forms. The most famous is Elephant Rock, which looks like a giant elephant with its trunk touching the ground.\n\n**Old and New** – Visitors can walk through the mud-brick Old Town and then attend a concert at Maraya, a huge building covered in mirrors that reflect the desert.\n\nToday, Saudi Arabia is working to preserve AlUla, so that future explorers can discover its secrets too.","words":230,"limit":153}],"time_limit_sec":153},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS3-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Setting → Effect Chart","instructions":"Organizer: Write a feature of the setting and what happens because of it.","items":[{"q":"Story – narrow, twisting alleys with high walls","answer":"Layla can't see far or find landmarks, so she gets lost."},{"q":"Story – sunset and the call to the Maghrib prayer","answer":"It is getting dark, so Layla feels more worried; lights come on in the souq."},{"q":"Article – AlUla on the Incense Road","answer":"Many traders pass through, so kingdoms like Dadan and Lihyan grow rich."},{"q":"Article – soft sandstone cliffs","answer":"The Nabataeans can carve more than 100 grand tombs into the rock at Hegra."}]}],"game":{"name":"Setting Detectives","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Map detective: the child taps a place on a map of Saudi Arabia (Al-Balad or AlUla), reads a setting feature and drags it to its effect. Correct matches stamp a passport page.","Teams get mixed setting and effect cards.","They match each setting feature to its effect.","They say a sentence with because or so.","They place the pair on the right place on the map.","Each correct match = 1 point; a correct because/so sentence = 1 bonus point."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What does the *setting* include?","answer":"where and when something happens, plus special features","explanation":"Setting.","explanation_ar":"المكان والزمان والسمات.","options":["only the title","where and when something happens, plus special features","only the characters' names","the last sentence"],"dok":1,"skill":"setting"},{"q":"What is *sandstone*?","answer":"a soft rock made of sand pressed together","explanation":"Glossary.","explanation_ar":"الحجر الرملي.","options":["a soft rock made of sand pressed together","a kind of mirror","a desert animal","a sweet smell"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*Ancient writings cover the cliffs.* *Ancient* means ___.","answer":"very, very old","explanation":"Ancient.","explanation_ar":"قديم جدًا.","options":["brand new","very small","very, very old","very colorful"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"What people built Petra in Jordan and the tombs at Hegra?","answer":"the Nabataeans","explanation":"Hegra.","explanation_ar":"الأنباط.","options":["the Nabataeans","the Romans","the Bedouin of today","the people of Jeddah"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"Which sentence about the tombs is correct?","answer":"The tombs were carved about 2,000 years ago.","explanation":"Passive.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["The tombs carved about 2,000 years ago.","The tombs was carved about 2,000 years ago.","The tombs were carve about 2,000 years ago.","The tombs were carved about 2,000 years ago."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Why is Jabal Ikmah called an *open library*?","answer":"Hundreds of ancient writings cover its cliffs.","explanation":"Open Library.","explanation_ar":"لكثرة النقوش القديمة.","options":["It has a library building.","It sells books.","Hundreds of ancient writings cover its cliffs.","It is open every day."],"dok":2,"skill":"key details"},{"q":"Which is a SETTING → EFFECT sentence?","answer":"Because AlUla was on a trade route, its kingdoms grew rich.","explanation":"Setting → effect.","explanation_ar":"جملة أثر المكان.","options":["AlUla is beautiful.","I like Elephant Rock.","Maraya has mirrors.","Because AlUla was on a trade route, its kingdoms grew rich."],"dok":2,"skill":"setting and effect"},{"q":"Why does the article end with *so that future explorers can discover its secrets too*?","answer":"to explain why preserving AlUla matters","explanation":"Author's purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليبيّن أهمية الحفاظ.","options":["to tell a story about explorers","to sell tickets","to explain why preserving AlUla matters","to describe Elephant Rock"],"dok":2,"skill":"author's purpose"},{"q":"How did the TIME of day add to Layla's problem in the story?","answer":"It was sunset, so it was getting dark and she felt more worried.","explanation":"Time setting.","explanation_ar":"الغروب زاد قلقها.","options":["It was noon, so it was too hot.","It was midnight, so the souq was closed.","It was early morning, so nobody was there.","It was sunset, so it was getting dark and she felt more worried."],"dok":3,"skill":"setting and effect"},{"q":"What do BOTH texts suggest about places?","answer":"Where you are can change what happens to people.","explanation":"Synthesis.","explanation_ar":"المكان يغيّر ما يحدث للناس.","options":["Old places are boring.","Where you are can change what happens to people.","Only cities have history.","Deserts have no history."],"dok":3,"skill":"compare texts"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'sandstone': golden sandstone cliffs"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'trade route': a camel caravan on a desert road"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'frankincense': pieces of frankincense in a bowl"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'tomb': a carved rock tomb with a doorway"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'carve': a hand carving stone"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'ancient': old writings on a rock"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reflect': a mirrored building showing the desert"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-01","prompt":"A carved Nabataean tomb at Hegra."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-02","prompt":"Elephant Rock and the mirrored Maraya building."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS3-03","prompt":"A setting and effect chart."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V01","script":"sandstone. The tombs were carved into sandstone."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V02","script":"trade route. AlUla was on an old trade route."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V03","script":"frankincense. Traders carried frankincense to faraway lands."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V04","script":"tomb. The Nabataeans carved tombs into the rocks."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V05","script":"carve. Workers carved doorways into the cliffs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V06","script":"ancient. Ancient writings cover the cliffs."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-V07","script":"reflect. The mirrors reflect the desert."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-T1","script":"In the northwest of Saudi Arabia, golden sandstone mountains rise above a green valley of palm trees. This is AlUla, a region so full of history that people call it \"a museum without walls.\" **A Crossroads of Kingdoms** – Thousands of years ago, AlUla lay on the Incense Road, a trade route that carried frankincense, spices and other goods between southern Arabia and the lands around the Mediterranean Sea. Because so many traders passed through, kingdoms such as Dadan and Lihyan grew rich here. **Hegra** – About 2,000 years ago, the Nabataeans – the same people who built Petra in Jordan – carved more than 100 grand tombs into the soft sandstone at Hegra. In 2008, Hegra became the first UNESCO World Heritage Site in Saudi Arabia. **An Open Library** – At Jabal Ikmah, hundreds of ancient writings cover the cliffs. Experts call it an \"open library\" because it shows how people wrote long ago. **Nature's Sculptures** – Over millions of years, wind and water have shaped the rocks into amazing forms. The most famous is Elephant Rock, which looks like a giant elephant with its trunk touching the ground. **Old and New** – Visitors can walk through the mud-brick Old Town and then attend a concert at Maraya, a huge building covered in mirrors that reflect the desert. Today, Saudi Arabia is working to preserve AlUla, so that future explorers can discover its secrets too."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS3-VID","script":"Golden cliffs, a green valley, rocks shaped like animals… Welcome to AlUla, a museum without walls! In the northwest of Saudi Arabia, golden sandstone mountains rise… Setting is more than a place! It can cause events. Because AlUla was on a trade route, kingdoms grew rich. Remember Layla in Old Jeddah? The high walls and twisting alleys made her get lost! Every place has a story. What will you discover?"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS4","title":"Writing Skill: Writing a strong hook","lesson_type":"writing_skill","objectives":["recognize five kinds of hooks: a question, a surprising fact, a sound word, dialogue and a vivid action or description","write a strong hook that connects to the topic of a travel narrative","replace weak openings (One day…, My name is…, This story is about…)"],"objectives_ar":["التعرف على خمسة أنواع من جمل الجذب: سؤال، حقيقة مدهشة، كلمة صوتية، حوار، وصف أو حدث حي.","كتابة جملة جذب قوية مرتبطة بموضوع سرد الرحلة.","استبدال البدايات الضعيفة."],"success_criteria":["I can name the type of hook a writer uses.","I can write at least two different hooks for the same topic.","My hook connects to the rest of the paragraph."],"success_criteria_ar":["أستطيع تسمية نوع جملة الجذب التي يستخدمها الكاتب.","أستطيع كتابة جملتي جذب مختلفتين على الأقل للموضوع نفسه.","جملة الجذب لديّ مرتبطة ببقية الفقرة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"hook","pos":"noun","ipa":"/hʊk/","definition":"the first sentence or two that grab the reader's attention","arabic":"جملة الجذب / التشويق","example":"Start your story with a strong hook.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"cable car","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkeɪbəl kɑr/","definition":"a small cabin that hangs from a wire and carries people up a mountain","arabic":"عربة تلفريك","example":"We rode a cable car up the mountain.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"fog","pos":"noun","ipa":"/fɔɡ/","definition":"a thick cloud close to the ground","arabic":"ضباب","example":"The villages disappeared in the fog.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"escape","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɪˈskeɪp/","definition":"to get away from something unpleasant","arabic":"يهرب من","example":"We escaped the summer heat.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"baboon","pos":"noun","ipa":"/bæˈbun/","definition":"a large monkey with a dog-like face","arabic":"قرد البابون","example":"Baboons played on the rocks.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"vivid","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈvɪvɪd/","definition":"very clear and full of detail, like a picture","arabic":"حيّ / نابض بالتفاصيل","example":"Use vivid details in your hook.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"onomatopoeia","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˌɑnəˌmɑtəˈpiə/","definition":"a word that sounds like the noise it describes (whoosh, splash, crunch)","arabic":"محاكاة الصوت","example":"Whoosh! is an example of onomatopoeia.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **hook** is the opening of your writing. Like a fishing hook, it **catches** the reader so he or she wants to keep reading.\n\nFive kinds of hooks: (1) a **question** – *Have you ever stood inside a cloud?* (2) a **surprising fact** – *Some mountains in Saudi Arabia are almost 3,000 meters high.* (3) a **sound word** – *Whoosh!* (4) **dialogue** – *\"Hold on tight!\" Dad shouted.* (5) a **vivid action or description** – *A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face.*\n\nAvoid **weak openings**: *One day I went…*, *My name is…*, *This story is about…*, *Hello, I will tell you…*. After the hook, **connect** to your topic: tell the reader **where** you are and **what** the writing is about.","examples":["Weak: *One day, I went to AlUla.* → Strong (description): ***Golden cliffs rose around us like the walls of a giant castle.***","Question: ***Have you ever seen a rock that looks exactly like an elephant?***","Surprising fact: ***More than 100 tombs at Hegra were carved by hand about 2,000 years ago.***","Sound word: ***Splash! The Red Sea was so clear that I could see fish swimming around my feet.***","Dialogue: ***\"Don't look down!\" my brother laughed as the cable car swung in the wind.***","Action: ***I grabbed my sketchbook and raced toward the old wooden balconies.***","Combination (Sami's model): ***Whoosh!*** + vivid detail + ***Was I flying?***","Connect after the hook: ***No – I was standing on a mountain near Abha.***","Weak: *This story is about my trip.* → It tells instead of grabbing attention."],"common_errors":["✗ One day I went to Abha. → ✓ Whoosh! A cold cloud rolled over my face. (Grab attention first.)","✗ My name is Sami and I will tell you about my trip. → ✓ \"Hold on tight!\" Dad shouted as the cable car moved. (Don't introduce yourself.)","✗ A hook that has nothing to do with the topic. → ✓ Make the hook connect to your trip. (Hook → topic.)","✗ Have you ever been to Abha. → ✓ Have you ever been to Abha? (A question needs **?**.)","✗ Whoosh a cloud rolled over my face. → ✓ Whoosh! A cloud rolled over my face. (Sound words often take **!**.)","✗ Using five hooks in a row. → ✓ One or two hooks are enough. (Don't overdo it.)"],"arabic":"جملة الجذب هي بداية الكتابة؛ تجذب القارئ كما يجذب الخطاف السمكة، فيرغب في متابعة القراءة.\n\nأنواعها: سؤال، حقيقة مدهشة، كلمة صوتية، حوار، وصف أو حدث حي.\n\nتجنب البدايات الضعيفة مثل: ذات يوم ذهبت…، اسمي…، هذه القصة عن…\n\nبعد جملة الجذب اربطها بالموضوع: أين أنت؟ وعمّ تكتب؟"},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"One day I went to Abha… Zzz. Boring! Let's catch the reader with a HOOK!","on_screen":"Writing a Strong Hook 🎣","visual":"A fishing hook pulls a sleepy reader awake."},{"time":"00:25–01:20","narration":"Whoosh! A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face…","on_screen":"(Sami's model)","visual":"Sami in fog on a mountain near Abha; a cable car; baboons on rocks."},{"time":"01:20–02:30","narration":"Five hooks: a question, a surprising fact, a sound word, dialogue, a vivid action!","on_screen":"? · ! · Whoosh! · \"…\" · 🎬","visual":"Five fishing lines each pull up a different hook example."},{"time":"02:30–03:05","narration":"Avoid weak openings: One day…, My name is…, This story is about…","on_screen":"Weak ❌ → Strong ✅","visual":"Weak sentences crumble and strong ones appear."},{"time":"03:05–03:30","narration":"After the hook, connect to your topic: where are you, and what happened?","on_screen":"Hook → Topic","visual":"An arrow from the hook to a map pin."}],"captions_ar":["ذات يوم ذهبت إلى أبها… ممل! لنجذب القارئ بجملة جذب!","ووووش! مرّت سحابة باردة رطبة على وجهي…","خمسة أنواع: سؤال، حقيقة مدهشة، كلمة صوتية، حوار، حدث حي!","تجنب البدايات الضعيفة: ذات يوم…، اسمي…، هذه القصة عن…","بعد جملة الجذب اربطها بموضوعك: أين أنت؟ وماذا حدث؟"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, lively male teacher, speed 0.95. Sami reads his model."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"The Day I Walked Above the Clouds (by Sami)","body":"Whoosh! A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face, and suddenly I couldn't see my own hands. Was I flying? No – I was standing on a mountain near Abha, more than 2,000 meters above the sea.\n\nLast summer, my family escaped the heat of Riyadh and drove south to the Asir region. On our first morning, we rode a cable car up the mountain. Below us, green valleys and tiny villages appeared and disappeared in the fog. At the top, the air was so cool that I needed a jacket in July! We drank hot tea and watched baboons play on the rocks. That trip taught me that Saudi Arabia is not only a land of sand – it also has mountains with their heads in the clouds.","word_count":128,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS4-1","genre":"model text","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["**Hook 1 – a sound word:** *Whoosh!* – it puts the reader right in the moment.","**Hook 2 – a vivid detail:** *A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face* – the reader can feel it.","**Hook 3 – a question:** *Was I flying?* – it makes the reader curious.","**The answer + setting:** *No – I was standing on a mountain near Abha* – the hook connects to the topic.","**Paragraph 2:** tells the trip in order (*Last summer, On our first morning, At the top*).","**Ending:** *That trip taught me …* – what the writer learned; it links back to the clouds in the hook."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS4-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read Sami's model. Choose the best answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ نموذج سامي واختر أفضل إجابة.","items":[{"q":"Where was Sami standing when the cloud rolled over his face?","answer":"on a mountain near Abha","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"على جبل قرب أبها.","options":["on a beach in Jeddah","in the old souq","on a boat","on a mountain near Abha"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why did Sami's family drive to the Asir region?","answer":"to escape the heat of Riyadh","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"للهروب من حر الرياض.","options":["to escape the heat of Riyadh","to visit a museum","to buy a jacket","to see the sea"],"dok":1,"skill":"cause and effect","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"How did the family get up the mountain?","answer":"by cable car","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"بعربة التلفريك.","options":["by camel","on foot","by cable car","by plane"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What surprised Sami at the top?","answer":"He needed a jacket in July.","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"احتاج معطفًا في يوليو.","options":["It was very hot.","There were no animals.","It was raining sand.","He needed a jacket in July."],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which animals did the family watch?","answer":"baboons","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"قرود البابون.","options":["camels","falcons","baboons","fish"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did Sami learn from the trip?","answer":"Saudi Arabia also has high, cool mountains, not only sand.","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"في السعودية جبال باردة لا رمال فقط.","options":["Cable cars are dangerous.","Saudi Arabia also has high, cool mountains, not only sand.","Riyadh is cold in summer.","Baboons are friendly pets."],"dok":2,"skill":"theme","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS4-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Hooks. Choose.","instructions_ar":"جمل الجذب: اختر.","items":[{"q":"What kind of hook is *Whoosh!*?","answer":"a sound word","explanation":"Onomatopoeia.","explanation_ar":"كلمة صوتية.","options":["a sound word","a question","a surprising fact","dialogue"],"dok":1,"skill":"hook types","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What kind of hook is *Have you ever seen a rock that looks like an elephant?*","answer":"a question","explanation":"Question.","explanation_ar":"سؤال.","options":["dialogue","a sound word","a weak opening","a question"],"dok":1,"skill":"hook types","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is a WEAK opening for a travel story about AlUla?","answer":"This story is about my trip to AlUla.","explanation":"Weak opening.","explanation_ar":"بداية ضعيفة.","options":["Golden cliffs rose around us like castle walls.","This story is about my trip to AlUla.","\"Look up!\" Dad whispered as we stood below the giant tomb.","Have you ever walked through a museum without walls?"],"dok":1,"skill":"weak openings","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which hook about Hegra is a SURPRISING FACT?","answer":"More than 100 tombs at Hegra were carved by hand about 2,000 years ago.","explanation":"Fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة مدهشة.","options":["More than 100 tombs at Hegra were carved by hand about 2,000 years ago.","I like old places.","Crunch! My shoes stepped on the sand.","\"Come on!\" said my sister."],"dok":2,"skill":"hook types","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which dialogue hook about the cable car is punctuated correctly?","answer":"\"Don't look down!\" my brother laughed.","explanation":"Dialogue punctuation.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["\"Don't look down\"! my brother laughed.","\"Don't look down!\" my brother laughed.","Don't look down! \"my brother laughed.\"","\"Don't look down!\" My brother laughed."],"dok":2,"skill":"dialogue hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"After the hook *Splash! Something cold grabbed my foot*, which sentence BEST connects to the topic?","answer":"It was only a wave on the beach at Umluj, where my family spent our holiday.","explanation":"Hook → topic.","explanation_ar":"تربط جملة الجذب بالموضوع.","options":["My favorite color is blue.","I have two brothers.","It was only a wave on the beach at Umluj, where my family spent our holiday.","Splash! Splash! Splash!"],"dok":3,"skill":"connecting hooks","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS4-A3","type":"gap_fill","stage":"freer","instructions":"Freer practice: Complete the hooks with the word bank.","instructions_ar":"أكمل جمل الجذب من بنك الكلمات.","items":[{"q":"________! The wave crashed over our boat near the coral reef.","answer":"Splash","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"كلمة صوتية.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Have you ________ walked between walls taller than a palm tree?","answer":"ever","explanation":"Question hook.","explanation_ar":"سؤال.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"\"Hold on ________!\" Dad shouted as the cable car swung.","answer":"tight","explanation":"Dialogue hook.","explanation_ar":"حوار.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Golden cliffs rose around us like the walls of a giant ________.","answer":"castle","explanation":"Vivid description.","explanation_ar":"وصف حي.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"More than ________ tombs at Hegra were carved by hand.","answer":"100","explanation":"Surprising fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة.","stage":"freer"},{"q":"I ________ my sketchbook and raced toward the old balconies.","answer":"grabbed","explanation":"Action hook.","explanation_ar":"حدث.","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS4-A4","type":"open","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write hooks.","instructions_ar":"اكتب جمل الجذب.","items":[{"q":"Change this weak opening into a QUESTION hook: *One day, I went to the Red Sea.*","answer":"Have you ever swum with fish as bright as rainbows?","explanation":"Question hook.","explanation_ar":"سؤال.","dok":2,"skill":"writing hooks","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a SOUND WORD hook for a trip to the desert.","answer":"Crunch! My sandals sank into the warm sand of the dune.","explanation":"Sound hook.","explanation_ar":"كلمة صوتية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing hooks","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a DIALOGUE hook for a visit to Al-Balad.","answer":"\"Don't get lost in the alleys!\" Mother warned as we stepped into Old Jeddah.","explanation":"Dialogue hook.","explanation_ar":"حوار.","dok":2,"skill":"writing hooks","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write a two-sentence opening: a hook + a sentence that connects to your topic.","answer":"Have you ever stood in a cloud? Last summer, I did – on a mountain in the Asir region.","explanation":"Hook + connection.","explanation_ar":"جذب + ربط.","dok":3,"skill":"connecting hooks","stage":"production"}]}],"game":{"name":"Hook, Line and Sinker","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Fishing pond: openings swim across the screen as fish; the child 'catches' only strong hooks and names their type. Weak openings that are caught make the line break.","Teams take turns 'fishing' one paper fish.","They read the opening aloud.","If it is a strong hook, they name its type and keep the fish.","If it is a weak opening, they must rewrite it as a hook to keep the fish.","Each correct type = 1 point; each good rewrite = 2 points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *hook*?","answer":"the opening that grabs the reader's attention","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"جملة الجذب.","options":["the last sentence","a list of facts","the title only","the opening that grabs the reader's attention"],"dok":1,"skill":"hook types"},{"q":"Which word is an example of onomatopoeia?","answer":"splash","explanation":"Sound word.","explanation_ar":"splash.","options":["valley","splash","souvenir","region"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary"},{"q":"*The villages disappeared in the **fog**.* *Fog* is ___.","answer":"a thick cloud close to the ground","explanation":"Fog.","explanation_ar":"ضباب.","options":["a strong wind","a heavy rain","a thick cloud close to the ground","a hot sun"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"Which hook about a desert trip uses a VIVID description?","answer":"The sand glowed orange, and the dunes rolled away like waves.","explanation":"Vivid description.","explanation_ar":"وصف حي.","options":["I went to the desert.","The sand glowed orange, and the dunes rolled away like waves.","The desert is a place.","This story is about the desert."],"dok":2,"skill":"hook types"},{"q":"Which question hook is punctuated correctly?","answer":"Have you ever slept under a sky full of stars?","explanation":"Capital + ?","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["Have you ever slept under a sky full of stars?","Have you ever slept under a sky full of stars.","have you ever slept under a sky full of stars?","Have you ever slept, under a sky full of stars"],"dok":2,"skill":"punctuation"},{"q":"Why should you avoid *My name is Sami, and I will tell you about my trip*?","answer":"It tells instead of grabbing the reader's attention.","explanation":"Weak opening.","explanation_ar":"لأنها لا تجذب القارئ.","options":["It is too exciting.","It uses a sound word.","It tells instead of grabbing the reader's attention.","It is a question."],"dok":2,"skill":"weak openings"},{"q":"What should come right AFTER a hook?","answer":"a sentence that connects to the topic (where and what)","explanation":"Connection.","explanation_ar":"جملة تربط بالموضوع.","options":["another five hooks","the closing","a sentence that connects to the topic (where and what)","a list of new words"],"dok":2,"skill":"connecting hooks"},{"q":"Which hook BEST fits a story about getting lost in Old Jeddah?","answer":"Every lane looked the same, and my family was gone.","explanation":"Hook fits topic.","explanation_ar":"يناسب الموضوع.","options":["Every lane looked the same, and my family was gone.","Elephants are big animals.","The Red Sea is salty.","Baboons live in Asir."],"dok":3,"skill":"connecting hooks"},{"q":"Sami's ending says Asir has *mountains with their heads in the clouds*. How does this connect to his hook?","answer":"It links back to the cloud in his first sentence.","explanation":"Circular ending.","explanation_ar":"يعود إلى سحابة البداية.","options":["It introduces a new topic.","It repeats the date.","It asks a new question.","It links back to the cloud in his first sentence."],"dok":3,"skill":"author's craft"},{"q":"Which hook uses a SURPRISING FACT about Saudi Arabia?","answer":"Some mountains in Saudi Arabia are almost 3,000 meters high.","explanation":"Fact.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة مدهشة.","options":["Saudi Arabia is a country.","Some mountains in Saudi Arabia are almost 3,000 meters high.","I live in Saudi Arabia.","Saudi Arabia has cities."],"dok":2,"skill":"hook types"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'hook': a fishing hook catching a book"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'cable car': a cable car above green mountains"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'fog': mountains in thick fog"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'escape': a family driving away from a hot city"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'baboon': baboons sitting on rocks"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'vivid': a bright, detailed painting"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'onomatopoeia': comic-style sound words"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-01","prompt":"Sami standing in the clouds on a mountain in Asir."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-02","prompt":"Five kinds of hooks on a fishing line."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS4-03","prompt":"A weak opening and a strong hook."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V01","script":"hook. Start your story with a strong hook."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V02","script":"cable car. We rode a cable car up the mountain."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V03","script":"fog. The villages disappeared in the fog."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V04","script":"escape. We escaped the summer heat."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V05","script":"baboon. Baboons played on the rocks."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V06","script":"vivid. Use vivid details in your hook."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-V07","script":"onomatopoeia. Whoosh! is an example of onomatopoeia."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-T1","script":"Whoosh! A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face, and suddenly I couldn't see my own hands. Was I flying? No – I was standing on a mountain near Abha, more than 2,000 meters above the sea. Last summer, my family escaped the heat of Riyadh and drove south to the Asir region. On our first morning, we rode a cable car up the mountain. Below us, green valleys and tiny villages appeared and disappeared in the fog. At the top, the air was so cool that I needed a jacket in July! We drank hot tea and watched baboons play on the rocks. That trip taught me that Saudi Arabia is not only a land of sand – it also has mountains with their heads in the clouds."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS4-VID","script":"One day I went to Abha… Zzz. Boring! Let's catch the reader with a HOOK! Whoosh! A cold, wet cloud rolled over my face… Five hooks: a question, a surprising fact, a sound word, dialogue, a vivid action! Avoid weak openings: One day…, My name is…, This story is about… After the hook, connect to your topic: where are you, and what happened?"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5","title":"Writing Workshop: A two-paragraph travel narrative","lesson_type":"writing_workshop","objectives":["plan a two-paragraph travel narrative with the Travel Narrative Planner","write 120–150 words: a hook and setting paragraph, then an events and ending paragraph","use time transitions, vivid details and a reflective ending; revise, edit and publish in a class travel magazine"],"objectives_ar":["التخطيط لسرد رحلة من فقرتين بمخطط سرد الرحلة.","كتابة 120–150 كلمة: فقرة للجذب والمكان، ثم فقرة للأحداث والخاتمة.","استخدام كلمات الانتقال الزمنية والتفاصيل الحية وخاتمة تأملية، ثم التحسين والنشر في مجلة رحلات الصف."],"success_criteria":["My first paragraph starts with a strong hook and tells where, when and who.","My second paragraph tells 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details.","My ending tells what I felt or learned, and my narrative is 120–150 words."],"success_criteria_ar":["فقرتي الأولى تبدأ بجملة جذب قوية وتذكر المكان والزمان والأشخاص.","فقرتي الثانية تروي 2–3 أحداث بالترتيب مع كلمات انتقال وتفاصيل حية.","خاتمتي تذكر ما شعرت به أو تعلمته، وسردي 120–150 كلمة."],"map_area":"Language Usage: Write, Revise Texts for Purpose and Audience (cross-strand)","vocabulary":[{"word":"snorkel","pos":"noun / verb","ipa":"/ˈsnɔrkəl/","definition":"a tube for breathing while swimming face down; to swim with one","arabic":"أنبوب التنفس / يغطس سطحيًا","example":"We snorkeled over the coral reef.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V01","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V01","tier":"core"},{"word":"coral reef","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈkɔrəl rif/","definition":"a hard, colorful structure in warm seas made by tiny sea animals","arabic":"شعاب مرجانية","example":"Colorful fish live on the coral reef.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V02","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V02","tier":"core"},{"word":"glide","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ɡlaɪd/","definition":"to move smoothly and quietly","arabic":"ينساب / ينزلق بسلاسة","example":"A turtle glided past us.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V03","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V03","tier":"core"},{"word":"nibble","pos":"verb","ipa":"/ˈnɪbəl/","definition":"to take very small bites","arabic":"يقضم","example":"A fish nibbled my toes.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V04","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V04","tier":"core"},{"word":"wrinkled","pos":"adjective","ipa":"/ˈrɪŋkəld/","definition":"with many small lines","arabic":"متجعّد","example":"My fingers were wrinkled from the water.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V05","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V05","tier":"core"},{"word":"narrative","pos":"noun","ipa":"/ˈnærətɪv/","definition":"a piece of writing that tells a story","arabic":"سرد / قصة","example":"Write a travel narrative about a trip.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V06","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V06","tier":"core"},{"word":"reflect","pos":"verb","ipa":"/rɪˈflɛkt/","definition":"to think carefully about something that happened","arabic":"يتأمّل","example":"In the ending, reflect on what you learned.","image_id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V07","audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V07","tier":"core"}],"explanation":{"text":"A **travel narrative** is a true story (or a realistic one) about a trip. It uses **first person** (I, we) and the **past tense**.\n\n**Paragraph 1:** start with a **strong hook** (sound word, question, dialogue, fact or vivid detail), then tell the **setting**: **where** you went, **when** and **who** was with you. **Paragraph 2:** tell **2–3 events in order** with **time transitions** (First, Then, After that, At the end of the day) and **vivid details**, then finish with a **reflective ending**: what you **felt** or **learned**.\n\nKeep it **120–150 words**. Start a **new paragraph** when you move from the setting to the events. Indent the first line of each paragraph.","examples":["Hook (sound + detail): ***Splash! The water near Umluj was so clear that I could see a yellow fish nibbling my toes.***","Hook (question): ***Was I in a giant aquarium?***","Setting: ***No – I was on my first snorkeling trip on the Red Sea coast with my uncle and my cousin Hamza.***","New paragraph + event 1: ***First, our guide showed us how to breathe through the snorkel and float calmly.***","Event 2 with vivid detail: ***Then we swam above a coral reef full of purple, orange and blue corals.***","Event 3 with a simile: ***A turtle glided past us slowly, as if it owned the sea.***","Time transition: ***At the end of the afternoon, my fingers were wrinkled, but I didn't want to leave.***","Reflective ending: ***That trip taught me that our country's coastline hides a whole colorful world under the waves, and we must protect it.***","Weak ending: *Then we went home. The end.* → no feeling, no lesson."],"common_errors":["✗ One long paragraph. → ✓ Two paragraphs: setting, then events + ending. (New idea, new paragraph.)","✗ No setting: the reader doesn't know where you are. → ✓ Add where, when and who after the hook. (Setting first.)","✗ Mixing tenses: I swim … then I saw … → ✓ I swam … then I saw … (Past tense all through.)","✗ Listing ten events. → ✓ Choose 2–3 events with details. (Quality, not quantity.)","✗ We went to Umluj in spring holiday. → ✓ We went to Umluj during the spring holiday. (**during** + a period of time.)","✗ Ending with 'The end.' → ✓ End with what you felt or learned. (Reflect!)","✗ Writing 300 words. → ✓ Keep it 120–150 words. (Check the length.)"],"arabic":"سرد الرحلة قصة حقيقية أو واقعية عن رحلة، بضمير المتكلم وبالزمن الماضي.\n\nالفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب قوية، ثم المكان والزمان ومن كان معك. الفقرة الثانية: 2–3 أحداث بالترتيب مع كلمات الانتقال والتفاصيل الحية، ثم خاتمة تأملية تذكر ما شعرت به أو تعلمته.\n\nالتزم بطول 120–150 كلمة، وابدأ فقرة جديدة عند الانتقال من المكان إلى الأحداث.\n\nحافظ على الزمن الماضي في السرد كله."},"video":{"script":[{"time":"00:00–00:25","narration":"Every trip is a story waiting to be told. Today, you are the travel writer!","on_screen":"My Travel Narrative ✈️📖","visual":"Omar opens a travel journal with photos and a map."},{"time":"00:25–01:05","narration":"Choose a trip card: Umluj, Abha, AlUla or Al-Balad – or a trip of your own!","on_screen":"Trip Memory Cards","visual":"Four cards with photos slide across a map of Saudi Arabia."},{"time":"01:05–02:10","narration":"Splash! The water near Umluj was so clear that I could see a yellow fish nibbling my toes…","on_screen":"(model narrative)","visual":"Omar snorkeling with Hamza and a guide; a coral reef; a turtle gliding."},{"time":"02:10–03:00","narration":"Paragraph 1: hook + setting. Paragraph 2: events in order + a reflective ending!","on_screen":"P1: Hook + Setting · P2: Events + Ending","visual":"Two paragraph blocks build up with labels."},{"time":"03:00–03:30","narration":"Plan, write, revise and publish your story in our class travel magazine!","on_screen":"Class Travel Magazine 📰","visual":"Student stories fly into a magazine cover titled 'Explorers'."}],"captions_ar":["كل رحلة قصة تنتظر أن تُروى. اليوم أنت كاتب الرحلات!","اختر بطاقة رحلة: أملج أو أبها أو العلا أو البلد، أو رحلة خاصة بك!","طرطشة! كانت المياه قرب أملج صافية حتى رأيت سمكة صفراء تقضم أصابع قدمي…","الفقرة الأولى: جذب + مكان. الفقرة الثانية: أحداث مرتبة + خاتمة تأملية!","خطّط واكتب وحسّن وانشر قصتك في مجلة رحلات الصف!"],"voice":"Narrator: American English, friendly female presenter, speed 0.95. Omar reads the model."},"texts":[{"type":"model","title":"Trip Memory Cards","body":"**Card A – Umluj, Red Sea coast** · When: spring holiday · Who: you, an uncle, a cousin · Events: learn to snorkel, swim over a coral reef, see a sea turtle · Details: clear water, colorful fish, warm sun · Feeling: amazed, a little nervous at first\n\n**Card B – Abha, Asir region** · When: summer · Who: your family · Events: cable car ride, walk in the fog, visit a colorful village · Details: cool air, green valleys, baboons · Feeling: surprised, peaceful\n\n**Card C – AlUla** · When: winter · Who: your class with a guide · Events: see the tombs at Hegra, climb near Elephant Rock at sunset, visit the Old Town · Details: golden cliffs, ancient writing, cold evening · Feeling: curious, proud\n\n**Card D – Al-Balad, Jeddah** · When: an evening in Ramadan · Who: your grandparents · Events: walk through the old lanes, buy a souvenir in the souq, eat sobia and samosas · Details: lanterns, coral-stone houses, busy stalls · Feeling: excited, happy","word_count":147,"audio_id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-T1","id":"TXT-RW-L05-U1-LS5-1","genre":"trip cards","role":"model","difficulty":"550L–750L","glossary":[],"annotations":["Choose **one card** (or a real trip of your own).","**Paragraph 1:** a strong **hook** + **where, when and who** (the setting).","**Paragraph 2:** **2–3 events in order** with vivid details, then an **ending** that tells what you **felt or learned**."]}],"activities":[{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-A1","type":"mcq","stage":"controlled","instructions":"Controlled practice: Read the trip cards and the model. Choose the answer.","instructions_ar":"اقرأ البطاقات والنموذج واختر الإجابة.","items":[{"q":"On Card C, who goes to AlUla?","answer":"your class with a guide","explanation":"Card C.","explanation_ar":"صفك مع مرشد.","options":["your grandparents","your class with a guide","your uncle and cousin","your family only"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Which card describes cool air, green valleys and baboons?","answer":"Card B – Abha","explanation":"Card B.","explanation_ar":"بطاقة أبها.","options":["Card B – Abha","Card A – Umluj","Card C – AlUla","Card D – Al-Balad"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"In the model, what did the guide teach first?","answer":"how to breathe through the snorkel and float calmly","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"التنفس بالأنبوب والطفو.","options":["how to catch fish","how to drive a boat","how to draw coral","how to breathe through the snorkel and float calmly"],"dok":1,"skill":"sequence","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What colors were the corals in the model?","answer":"purple, orange and blue","explanation":"Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"بنفسجي وبرتقالي وأزرق.","options":["black and white","only green","purple, orange and blue","gray and brown"],"dok":1,"skill":"key details","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"Why were the writer's fingers wrinkled?","answer":"He stayed in the water all afternoon.","explanation":"Inference.","explanation_ar":"بقي في الماء طويلًا.","options":["He stayed in the water all afternoon.","He touched the coral.","The water was very cold.","He was writing a lot."],"dok":2,"skill":"inference","stage":"controlled"},{"q":"What did the writer learn from the trip?","answer":"The coastline hides a colorful world that we must protect.","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"حماية العالم الملوّن تحت البحر.","options":["Snorkeling is boring.","Turtles are fast.","Uncles are good swimmers.","The coastline hides a colorful world that we must protect."],"dok":2,"skill":"theme","stage":"controlled"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-A2","type":"mcq","stage":"guided","instructions":"Guided practice: Plan the narrative. Choose.","instructions_ar":"خطّط للسرد: اختر.","items":[{"q":"Which opening is the STRONGEST hook for a trip to Abha (Card B)?","answer":"Whoosh! The cable car swung, and the valley disappeared in a cloud.","explanation":"Strong hook.","explanation_ar":"الأقوى.","options":["Whoosh! The cable car swung, and the valley disappeared in a cloud.","One day, we went to Abha.","My name is Badr, and this is my trip.","Abha is a city."],"dok":1,"skill":"hooks","stage":"guided"},{"q":"What should come right after the hook in Paragraph 1?","answer":"where, when and who (the setting)","explanation":"Structure.","explanation_ar":"المكان والزمان والأشخاص.","options":["the ending","a list of new words","where, when and who (the setting)","the title again"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Where should the SECOND paragraph begin?","answer":"when the narrative moves from the setting to the events","explanation":"Paragraphing.","explanation_ar":"عند الانتقال إلى الأحداث.","options":["after every sentence","in the middle of the hook","when the narrative moves from the setting to the events","after the ending"],"dok":2,"skill":"paragraphing","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which group of transitions is BEST for Paragraph 2?","answer":"First, Then, After that, At the end of the day","explanation":"Time order.","explanation_ar":"كلمات الانتقال الزمنية.","options":["First, Then, After that, At the end of the day","However, Although, Unlike","For example, Such as, Including","Dear, Best regards, P.S."],"dok":2,"skill":"transitions","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which sentence about the AlUla trip keeps the PAST tense?","answer":"We climbed near Elephant Rock at sunset.","explanation":"Past tense.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We climb near Elephant Rock at sunset yesterday.","We climbed near Elephant Rock at sunset.","We will climbed near Elephant Rock.","We climbing near Elephant Rock at sunset."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","stage":"guided"},{"q":"Which is the BEST reflective ending for a trip to Al-Balad (Card D)?","answer":"Walking through those old lanes made me proud of the people who built Jeddah long ago.","explanation":"Feeling + lesson.","explanation_ar":"خاتمة تأملية.","options":["Then we went home. The end.","Al-Balad has many stalls.","Sobia is a drink.","Walking through those old lanes made me proud of the people who built Jeddah long ago."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings","stage":"guided"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-A3","type":"organizer","stage":"freer","instructions":"Organizer: Plan your narrative with the Travel Narrative Planner.","instructions_ar":"خطط لسردك بمخطط سرد الرحلة.","items":[{"q":"Hook – write your hook and name its type.","answer":"Question: Have you ever touched a wall that is 2,000 years old?","explanation":"Hook.","explanation_ar":"جملة الجذب.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Paragraph 1 – where, when and who (notes).","answer":"AlUla; last winter; my class and a guide","explanation":"Setting.","explanation_ar":"المكان والزمان.","dok":1,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Paragraph 2 – 2–3 events in order with details (notes).","answer":"First – Hegra tombs, carved doorways; Then – Elephant Rock at sunset, golden light; After that – cold evening in the Old Town","explanation":"Events.","explanation_ar":"الأحداث.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"},{"q":"Ending – what you felt or learned (notes).","answer":"proud; AlUla's history belongs to all of us, and we must preserve it","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":2,"skill":"planning","stage":"freer"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-A4","type":"writing","stage":"production","instructions":"Writing: Write, revise, edit and publish your narrative.","instructions_ar":"اكتب سردك وحسّنه ودققه وانشره.","items":[{"q":"Write Paragraph 1: your hook and the setting.","answer":"Have you ever touched a wall that is 2,000 years old? Last winter, I did – on a class trip to AlUla with our guide, Mr. Fawaz.","explanation":"Paragraph 1.","explanation_ar":"الفقرة الأولى.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with details.","answer":"First, we visited the tombs at Hegra, where huge doorways were carved into golden rock. Then we watched the sun set behind Elephant Rock, which glowed orange like a lantern. After that, we walked through the cold, quiet Old Town.","explanation":"Paragraph 2 events.","explanation_ar":"أحداث الفقرة الثانية.","dok":2,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write your reflective ending.","answer":"That trip made me proud, because AlUla's history belongs to all of us, and we must preserve it for future explorers.","explanation":"Ending.","explanation_ar":"الخاتمة.","dok":3,"skill":"writing","stage":"production"},{"q":"Write one sentence you made more vivid after the Two-Partner Edit (before → after).","answer":"Before: We saw a big rock. After: We watched the sun set behind Elephant Rock, which glowed orange like a lantern.","explanation":"Revision.","explanation_ar":"تحسين.","dok":3,"skill":"revising","stage":"production","_w":{"checklist":[],"min":null,"max":null}}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-ORG","type":"organizer","organizer":"Travel Narrative Planner","instructions":"Organizer: Use a trip card or your own trip. Write short notes for each part.","items":[{"q":"Hook (type + words)","answer":"sound + question: 'Splash! … Was I in a giant aquarium?'"},{"q":"Paragraph 1 – setting: where, when, who","answer":"Umluj, Red Sea coast; spring holiday; my uncle and cousin Hamza; first snorkeling trip"},{"q":"Paragraph 2 – events in order (2–3) with details","answer":"First – guide teaches snorkeling; Then – coral reef, purple/orange/blue; a turtle glides past"},{"q":"Ending – what I felt or learned","answer":"fingers wrinkled, didn't want to leave; the coastline hides a colorful world we must protect"}]},{"id":"RW-L05-U1-LS5-WR","type":"writing","instructions":"Writing: Write a two-paragraph travel narrative (120–150 words) about a trip. Use a trip card or your own trip. Paragraph 1: a strong hook and the setting (where, when, who). Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details, and a reflective ending. Write a two-paragraph travel narrative (120–150 words) about a trip. Use a trip card or your own trip. Paragraph 1: a strong hook and the setting (where, when, who). Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details, and a reflective ending.","instructions_ar":"اكتب سرد رحلة من فقرتين (120–150 كلمة) مستعينًا ببطاقة رحلة أو برحلتك الخاصة. الفقرة الأولى: جملة جذب قوية والمكان والزمان والأشخاص. الفقرة الثانية: 2–3 أحداث بالترتيب مع كلمات انتقال وتفاصيل حية وخاتمة تأملية.","prompt":"Write a two-paragraph travel narrative (120–150 words) about a trip. Use a trip card or your own trip. Paragraph 1: a strong hook and the setting (where, when, who). Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details, and a reflective ending.","checklist":["I started with a strong hook (not One day… or My name is…).","Paragraph 1 tells where, when and who.","Paragraph 2 tells 2–3 events in order.","I used time transitions (First, Then, After that, At the end of the day).","I used vivid details and at least one simile or sound word.","I used the past tense and first person all through.","My ending tells what I felt or learned.","My narrative has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"rubric_id":"RW-RUBRIC-5T","min_words":120,"max_words":170,"organizer":"Travel Narrative Planner (hook – setting – events – ending)","items":[{"q":"Write a two-paragraph travel narrative (120–150 words) about a trip. Use a trip card or your own trip. Paragraph 1: a strong hook and the setting (where, when, who). Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details, and a reflective ending.","answer":"Splash! The water near Umluj was so clear that I could see a yellow fish nibbling my toes. Was I in a giant aquarium? No – I was on my first snorkeling trip on the Red Sea coast with my uncle and my cousin Hamza. (new paragraph) First, our guide showed us how to breathe through the snorkel and float calmly. Then we swam above a coral reef full of purple, orange and blue corals. A turtle glided past us slowly, as if it owned the sea. At the end of the afternoon, my fingers were wrinkled, but I didn't want to leave. That trip taught me that our country's coastline hides a whole colorful world under the waves, and we must protect it.","_w":{"checklist":["I started with a strong hook (not One day… or My name is…).","Paragraph 1 tells where, when and who.","Paragraph 2 tells 2–3 events in order.","I used time transitions (First, Then, After that, At the end of the day).","I used vivid details and at least one simile or sound word.","I used the past tense and first person all through.","My ending tells what I felt or learned.","My narrative has two paragraphs and 120–150 words, and I checked spelling and punctuation."],"min":120,"max":170}}]}],"game":{"name":"Travel Story Relay","mechanic":"timed_quiz","rules":["On this screen: answer the lesson questions as fast as you can.","In class: Journey builder: the child chooses a destination on the map, then drags the best hook, setting, events and ending into two paragraphs. A complete, well-ordered story earns a passport stamp for that destination.","Each team chooses a destination card.","Runner 1 chooses the best hook strip; Runner 2 the setting; Runner 3 the events in order; Runner 4 the ending.","The team decides where Paragraph 2 begins and reads the story aloud.","Other teams check: hook? setting? order? ending?","Each correct part = 2 points; the best hook in the class = 3 bonus points."],"data":[]},"exit_quiz":[{"q":"What is a *travel narrative*?","answer":"a story about a trip, told in the first person","explanation":"Genre.","explanation_ar":"قصة عن رحلة.","options":["a list of hotel prices","a story about a trip, told in the first person","a map of a city","a dictionary of travel words"],"dok":1,"skill":"genre"},{"q":"*A turtle **glided** past us.* *Glided* means ___.","answer":"moved smoothly and quietly","explanation":"Glide.","explanation_ar":"انساب بسلاسة.","options":["stopped suddenly","jumped high","bit hard","moved smoothly and quietly"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context"},{"q":"How many paragraphs should your travel narrative have?","answer":"two","explanation":"Task.","explanation_ar":"فقرتان.","options":["one","five","two","ten"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"What belongs in Paragraph 1 after the hook?","answer":"where, when and who","explanation":"Setting.","explanation_ar":"المكان والزمان والأشخاص.","options":["where, when and who","the lesson you learned","the last event","a list of souvenirs"],"dok":1,"skill":"organization"},{"q":"Which sentence about the snorkeling trip is correct?","answer":"We swam above a colorful coral reef.","explanation":"Irregular past: swam.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We swim above a colorful coral reef yesterday.","We swimmed above a colorful coral reef.","We swum above a colorful coral reef.","We swam above a colorful coral reef."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense"},{"q":"Which sentence uses *during* correctly?","answer":"We went to Umluj during the spring holiday.","explanation":"during + period.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We went to Umluj during in spring.","We went to Umluj during the spring holiday.","We went to Umluj during two days ago.","We went to Umluj in during the holiday."],"dok":2,"skill":"grammar"},{"q":"Which transition shows the LAST event of the day?","answer":"At the end of the afternoon,","explanation":"Time order.","explanation_ar":"في آخر النهار.","options":["At the end of the afternoon,","First,","Then,","Have you ever"],"dok":2,"skill":"transitions"},{"q":"Why should the ending be *reflective*?","answer":"It shows what the trip meant to the writer.","explanation":"Purpose.","explanation_ar":"ليُظهر معنى الرحلة للكاتب.","options":["It makes the narrative longer.","It repeats the hook word for word.","It shows what the trip meant to the writer.","It lists every place."],"dok":2,"skill":"endings"},{"q":"A partner says, *I don't know where you are until the end.* What should you fix?","answer":"Add the setting right after the hook.","explanation":"Revising.","explanation_ar":"أضف المكان بعد جملة الجذب.","options":["Remove the hook.","Add the setting right after the hook.","Add more events.","Write in the present tense."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"},{"q":"Your narrative is 260 words. What is the BEST way to shorten it?","answer":"Keep 2–3 main events and remove extra small details.","explanation":"Length.","explanation_ar":"أبقِ 2–3 أحداث.","options":["Delete the hook and the ending.","Write it all in one paragraph.","Remove all the transitions.","Keep 2–3 main events and remove extra small details."],"dok":3,"skill":"revising"}],"images":[{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V01","prompt":"Picture for the word 'snorkel': a child snorkeling over coral"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V02","prompt":"Picture for the word 'coral reef': a colorful coral reef"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V03","prompt":"Picture for the word 'glide': a sea turtle swimming smoothly"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V04","prompt":"Picture for the word 'nibble': a small fish near a foot"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V05","prompt":"Picture for the word 'wrinkled': wrinkled fingertips"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V06","prompt":"Picture for the word 'narrative': an open notebook with a story"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V07","prompt":"Picture for the word 'reflect': a child thinking and looking at photos"},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-01","prompt":"A class travel magazine cover."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-02","prompt":"Four trip memory cards."},{"id":"IMG-RW-L05-U1-LS5-03","prompt":"A suitcase-shaped narrative planner."}],"audio":[{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V01","script":"snorkel. We snorkeled over the coral reef."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V02","script":"coral reef. Colorful fish live on the coral reef."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V03","script":"glide. A turtle glided past us."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V04","script":"nibble. A fish nibbled my toes."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V05","script":"wrinkled. My fingers were wrinkled from the water."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V06","script":"narrative. Write a travel narrative about a trip."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-V07","script":"reflect. In the ending, reflect on what you learned."},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-T1","script":"**Card A – Umluj, Red Sea coast** · When: spring holiday · Who: you, an uncle, a cousin · Events: learn to snorkel, swim over a coral reef, see a sea turtle · Details: clear water, colorful fish, warm sun · Feeling: amazed, a little nervous at first **Card B – Abha, Asir region** · When: summer · Who: your family · Events: cable car ride, walk in the fog, visit a colorful village · Details: cool air, green valleys, baboons · Feeling: surprised, peaceful **Card C – AlUla** · When: winter · Who: your class with a guide · Events: see the tombs at Hegra, climb near Elephant Rock at sunset, visit the Old Town · Details: golden cliffs, ancient writing, cold evening · Feeling: curious, proud **Card D – Al-Balad, Jeddah** · When: an evening in Ramadan · Who: your grandparents · Events: walk through the old lanes, buy a souvenir in the souq, eat sobia and samosas · Details: lanterns, coral-stone houses, busy stalls · Feeling: excited, happy"},{"id":"AUD-RW-L05-U1-LS5-VID","script":"Every trip is a story waiting to be told. Today, you are the travel writer! Choose a trip card: Umluj, Abha, AlUla or Al-Balad – or a trip of your own! Splash! The water near Umluj was so clear that I could see a yellow fish nibbling my toes… Paragraph 1: hook + setting. Paragraph 2: events in order + a reflective ending! Plan, write, revise and publish your story in our class travel magazine!"}]}],"unit_test":[{"q":"Our plane landed in AlUla, the ___ of our winter trip.","answer":"destination","explanation":"Destination.","explanation_ar":"وجهة.","options":["souvenir","passport","coastline","destination"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"The tall clock tower is easy to see from far away. It is a famous ___.","answer":"landmark","explanation":"Landmark.","explanation_ar":"معلم.","options":["valley","voyage","guide","landmark"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Grandma bought a small wooden camel at the souq to remember the trip. It was a ___.","answer":"souvenir","explanation":"Souvenir.","explanation_ar":"تذكار.","options":["region","souvenir","heritage","landmark"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*The sailors' **voyage** lasted three weeks; the long journey took them across two seas.* What clue helps you understand *voyage*?","answer":"the synonym *journey*","explanation":"Synonym clue.","explanation_ar":"المرادف journey.","options":["the synonym *journey*","an antonym after *but*","an example after *such as*","there is no clue"],"dok":2,"skill":"context clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Old songs, traditional clothes and coral-stone houses are all part of our ___.","answer":"heritage","explanation":"Heritage.","explanation_ar":"التراث.","options":["passport","destination","heritage","valley"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Before maps on phones, sailors used the stars to **navigate**.* *Navigate* means ___.","answer":"find their way","explanation":"Context.","explanation_ar":"يجدوا طريقهم.","options":["sleep","find their way","catch fish","sing songs"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary in context","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"*Unlike the crowded beach, the small island was **secluded**.* *Secluded* means ___.","answer":"quiet and far from people","explanation":"Antonym clue: unlike crowded.","explanation_ar":"منعزل.","options":["quiet and far from people","full of people","very noisy","very expensive"],"dok":2,"skill":"antonym clues","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Workers repaired the old walls carefully to ___ the historic house for the future.","answer":"preserve","explanation":"Preserve.","explanation_ar":"يحافظوا على.","options":["discover","explore","preserve","navigate"],"dok":1,"skill":"vocabulary","_part":"Part A – Vocabulary"},{"q":"Which is the STRONGEST hook for a story about fog on a mountain?","answer":"In seconds, a white wall of fog swallowed the path in front of me.","explanation":"Vivid hook.","explanation_ar":"الأقوى.","options":["One day, I went to a mountain.","This story is about fog.","My name is Hamza, and I like hiking.","In seconds, a white wall of fog swallowed the path in front of me."],"dok":1,"skill":"hooks","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"What kind of hook is *Crack! A branch snapped under my boot*?","answer":"a sound word","explanation":"Onomatopoeia.","explanation_ar":"كلمة صوتية.","options":["a sound word","a question","a surprising fact","dialogue"],"dok":1,"skill":"hooks","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which hook about Ibn Battuta is a SURPRISING FACT?","answer":"Ibn Battuta traveled about 120,000 kilometers – without trains or planes!","explanation":"Fact hook.","explanation_ar":"حقيقة مدهشة.","options":["Ibn Battuta was a man.","Ibn Battuta traveled about 120,000 kilometers – without trains or planes!","This report is about Ibn Battuta.","I like reading about travelers."],"dok":2,"skill":"hooks","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"In a two-paragraph travel narrative about Al-Soudah, which sentence should START Paragraph 2?","answer":"First, we followed the trail through the juniper trees.","explanation":"Events begin Paragraph 2.","explanation_ar":"بداية الفقرة الثانية.","options":["Have you ever walked inside a cloud?","First, we followed the trail through the juniper trees.","Last summer, my family went to Abha.","My name is Hamza."],"dok":2,"skill":"paragraphing","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which sentence about the hike keeps the past tense?","answer":"We climbed slowly and followed the white arrows.","explanation":"Past tense.","explanation_ar":"صحيحة.","options":["We climb slowly and followed the white arrows.","We climbed slowly and follow the white arrows.","We climbed slowly and followed the white arrows.","We climbing slowly and following the white arrows."],"dok":2,"skill":"verb tense","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"},{"q":"Which is the BEST reflective ending for a story about getting lost in fog?","answer":"That day, I learned that staying calm is the first step to finding your way.","explanation":"Reflective ending.","explanation_ar":"خاتمة تأملية.","options":["That day, I learned that staying calm is the first step to finding your way.","Then we got in the car.","The fog was white.","The end."],"dok":3,"skill":"endings","_part":"Part D – Writing skills"}],"unit_writing":{"task":"Write a two-paragraph travel narrative (120–150 words) about a trip where the place or the weather surprised you or caused a problem. Paragraph 1: a strong hook and the setting (where, when, who). Paragraph 2: 2–3 events in order with time transitions and vivid details, and a reflective ending.","model":"Drip, drip, splash! Rain was falling on the desert, and the sand around our tent was turning dark. Last winter, my family camped near AlUla for the first time, and nobody expected rain. (new paragraph) First, we ran to cover the firewood and the food with a plastic sheet. Then we sat inside the tent and listened to the rain drumming on the roof like a hundred tiny horses. After an hour, the clouds moved away, and the whole valley smelled fresh and clean. Two days later, before we left, tiny green plants were pushing through the sand near our tent. That trip taught me that the desert is full of surprises, and even a problem like rain can bring something beautiful.","marks":20}});
